import{t as e}from"./links-ClIw6rLF.js";var t={id:`F1`,title:`Electrical Safety and Fundamentals`,level:`beginner`,stage:`foundation`,summary:`The mandatory first course: how electricity harms people, where energy is stored, safe isolation and proving dead, protection, earthing and safe workshop practice. Passing F1 (80 %) opens the practical laboratories.`,prerequisites:[],status:`published`,phase:7,competency:`CU-01`,modules:[{id:`F1.M1`,title:`Electricity and its hazards`,summary:`What voltage, current and resistance are, and how electricity injures people and starts fires. This is the foundation for every safety rule in the programme.`,lessons:[{id:`F1.M1.L1`,title:`Voltage, current, resistance and the circuit`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Describe voltage, current and resistance in everyday terms and give their units.`,`Explain why current flows only in a complete circuit, and why a person can become part of that circuit.`,`Use Ohm’s law, I = V / R, to estimate a current.`,`Explain why voltage is what makes electricity dangerous to touch, and current is what causes harm.`],introduction:`You do not need to be an engineer to understand why electricity is dangerous. You need three ideas — [[voltage]], [[current]] and [[resistance]] — and one rule: current flows only around a complete loop. Once you can picture these, every safety rule in this course makes sense: why we isolate, why we earth metal cases, why wet skin is dangerous, and why a fuse protects a cable.

This first lesson builds that picture. Course F2 then develops the same ideas into full circuit analysis.`,lecture:`### Current: the flow of charge

Electric [[current]] is a flow of electric [[charge]]. In a copper wire the moving charges are electrons. Copper is a [[conductor]] because its electrons are free to move. Current is measured in **amperes (A)**. One ampere is a flow of one coulomb of charge per second, which is about 6.24 × 10¹⁸ electrons every second.

| Current | Typical example |
|---|---|
| 0.02 A (20 mA) | An LED indicator |
| 0.5 A | A phone charger at the wall socket |
| 4 A | A 1 kW kettle on 230 V |
| 40 A | A 500 W inverter running from a 12 V battery |
| 1000 A or more | A 12 V battery short circuit |

### Voltage: the push

[[voltage|Voltage]] is the "push" that drives charge around a circuit. More precisely, it is the energy given to each coulomb of charge, measured in **volts (V)**. A 12 V battery gives 12 joules to every coulomb that passes through it. Voltage always exists *between two points*: the battery's two terminals, a mains socket's line and neutral, or a live wire and the earth you stand on.

### Resistance: the opposition

[[resistance|Resistance]] opposes current and is measured in **ohms (Ω)**. Copper wire has very little resistance. An [[insulator]] such as PVC or dry rubber has an enormous resistance. The human body lies in between, and its resistance changes greatly with conditions.

### Ohm's law

For many materials the current is proportional to the voltage:

**I = V / R**

Double the push and you double the flow; double the opposition and you halve it. This one equation explains most of electrical safety:

- A high voltage can drive a dangerous current through a body.
- A low resistance (wet skin, a metal floor) lets more current flow at the same voltage.
- A short circuit (R almost zero) draws an enormous current.

### The complete circuit

Current flows only around a **closed loop**: out from the source, through the load and back. Break the loop anywhere — open a switch, blow a fuse — and the current stops everywhere in the loop at once.

A person is injured when their body **completes a loop**. Two common ways:

- **Between two conductors.** Touching line and neutral at once, or a battery's positive and negative terminals.
- **Between a conductor and earth.** Most public supplies have their neutral connected to earth at the transformer. So touching just the line conductor while standing on the ground completes a loop through your body, the ground and the supply's earth connection. That is why "only touching one wire" is not safe.

### Two quantities, two dangers

- **Voltage determines whether a current can be driven *through you*.** Your body's resistance is relatively high, so it takes a significant voltage to push a harmful current through it.
- **Current determines the harm.** Current through the body causes shock. Large currents through wires and connections cause heating and fire.

This is why both "low-voltage" batteries and high-voltage mains are dangerous, in different ways: one can burn, the other can kill.`,technical:`**Ohm's law is a model, not a law of nature.** It holds well for metals at constant temperature. Many components do not obey it: diodes, lamps (whose filament resistance rises as it heats) and the human body (whose skin resistance falls as voltage rises and the skin breaks down). For safety estimates we still use I = V/R with a *typical* resistance, to get an order of magnitude.

**Conventional current versus electron flow.** By historical convention, current is drawn flowing from + to −. Electrons actually move the other way. All circuit analysis uses conventional current, and so does this programme.

**Power.** Electrical power is P = V·I, in watts. Combined with Ohm's law it becomes P = I²·R: the heating in a resistance rises with the square of the current. A wire carrying twice its rated current heats four times as fast. F2 develops this in full.

**AC and DC.** A battery gives direct current ([[dc]]), which flows one way. Mains gives alternating current ([[ac]]), which reverses 50 or 60 times a second. The safety principles in this course apply to both. Where the body's response differs, the difference is noted.`,diagrams:[{id:`circuit-loop`,caption:`A complete circuit, and the two ways a body can complete a loop: between two conductors, or between a conductor and earth.`}],formulas:[{name:`Ohm’s law`,expr:`I = V / R   (V = I · R,  R = V / I)`,where:[[`I`,`current`,`A`],[`V`,`voltage`,`V`],[`R`,`resistance`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Electrical power`,expr:`P = V · I = I² · R`,where:[[`P`,`power`,`W`]]},{name:`Current as charge flow`,expr:`I = Q / t`,where:[[`Q`,`charge`,`C`],[`t`,`time`,`s`]]}],examples:[{title:`Current through a heater element`,problem:`A heating element has a resistance of 26.5 Ω and is connected to 230 V. What current flows, and what power does it produce?`,steps:[`I = V / R = 230 / 26.5 = 8.68 A`,`P = V · I = 230 × 8.68 = 1996 W ≈ 2 kW`],answer:`About 8.7 A and 2 kW: a typical fan heater.`,check:`P = I²·R = 8.68² × 26.5 = 1997 W. ✓`},{title:`What resistance gives 1 A?`,problem:`What resistance would draw 1 A from (a) a 12 V battery and (b) a 230 V supply?`,steps:[`(a) R = V / I = 12 / 1 = 12 Ω`,`(b) R = 230 / 1 = 230 Ω`],answer:`At 230 V even a fairly high resistance such as 230 Ω passes 1 A. That is why mains can drive dangerous currents through things, including people, that a 12 V battery cannot.`},{title:`A short circuit`,problem:`A 12 V battery is shorted by a wire of 0.01 Ω (ignore the battery’s own resistance). What current would flow?`,steps:[`Ohm’s law: I = V / R`,`I = 12 / 0.01 = 1200 A`],answer:`1200 A. A real battery’s internal resistance limits it somewhat, but the current is still hundreds of amperes, enough to melt the wire. Lesson F1.M2.L1 returns to this.`}],applications:`Every electrical installation is designed around Ohm's law:

- **Cables** are sized so that the current they carry does not overheat them.
- **Fuses** open the loop when the current is too large.
- **Insulation** keeps the resistance between conductors and people very high.
- **Earthing** gives a fault current a low-resistance path, so that the protection operates quickly.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`“It’s only 12 volts, it can’t hurt.”`,correction:`A 12 V battery rarely gives a shock, but it can deliver hundreds of amperes into a short circuit and cause severe burns and fires.`},{mistake:`“If I touch only one wire, no current can flow.”`,correction:`The supply neutral is usually earthed, so a single live wire and the ground you stand on complete the loop.`},{mistake:`“Voltage flows through the wire.”`,correction:`Current flows; voltage is the push between two points that drives it.`}],demo:{title:`Ohm’s law with the body in the circuit`,text:`Set the voltage and the body resistance and see the resulting current, compared with published threshold ranges. Notice how strongly the current depends on the resistance of the skin and contact.`,link:e.lib(`Resistor: Ohm’s law and ratings`,`resistor`),embed:`body-current`},activities:[e.lib(`Fuse`,`fuse`),e.lib(`Protective earth`,`protective-earth`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current through a 46 Ω load on 230 V (A)?`,answer:5,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`I = 230/46 = 5 A.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What is needed for current to flow?`,options:[`A complete loop from the source and back`,`Only a high voltage`,`Only a thick wire`,`A switch that is open`],answer:0,explain:`Current flows only around a closed circuit.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why can touching only the live wire of a mains supply give a shock?`,options:[`The supply neutral is earthed, so the body and the ground complete the loop`,`Live wires attract current`,`It cannot`,`Because of the fuse`],answer:0,explain:`The ground is part of the circuit when the supply is earthed.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power drawn by a 12 Ω resistor on 12 V (W)?`,answer:12,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`I = 1 A; P = 12 × 1 = 12 W.`}],summary:[`Current (A) is the flow of charge; voltage (V) is the push between two points; resistance (Ω) opposes current.`,`Ohm’s law: I = V/R. Power: P = V·I = I²·R.`,`Current flows only in a complete loop; a body can complete a loop between two conductors or between a conductor and earth.`,`Voltage decides whether current can be driven through a person; current causes the harm.`,`Low-voltage sources can still deliver huge short-circuit currents.`],homework:{id:`F1.M1.L1.hw`,title:`Ohm’s law practice`,brief:`Use I = V/R and P = V·I.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A lamp draws 0.25 A from 12 V. Its resistance (Ω)?`,answer:48,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`R = 12/0.25 = 48 Ω.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power of that lamp (W)?`,answer:3,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`P = 12 × 0.25 = 3 W.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current drawn by a 2300 W kettle on 230 V (A)?`,answer:10,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`I = P/V = 2300/230 = 10 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain, using the idea of a complete loop, why a bird can sit on a single overhead line without harm but a person on a ladder touching the same line is in danger.`,model:`The bird touches only one conductor and nothing else, so there is no loop through its body and almost no voltage between its feet. The person on the ladder is also connected to the ground (through the ladder and their body), so the live line, the body and the earth form a loop with the earthed supply, and current flows through them.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M1.L2`,title:`How electric current harms the body`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Describe the effects of current through the body as current increases, using published threshold ranges.`,`Explain why body resistance, current path and duration change the danger.`,`Estimate body current for a given voltage and contact condition.`,`Explain why 30 mA residual current devices are used.`],introduction:`Electric shock is the passage of current through the body. The body runs on tiny electrical signals: nerves carry them, and the heart's rhythm is set by them. A current from outside, even a small one by electrical standards, interferes with those signals. This lesson explains what happens as the current increases, and which conditions make a shock more dangerous.

> The thresholds below are **approximate published ranges** from international studies of the effects of current on people (summarised in the IEC 60479 series). Individual people vary widely. Treat them as a guide to *orders of magnitude*, never as a "safe limit" to work near.`,lecture:`### Effects of current (AC, 50/60 Hz, hand to hand or hand to feet)

| Current through the body | Typical effect |
|---|---|
| About 0.5 mA | Threshold of perception: a slight tingle |
| A few mA | Painful; muscles start to react |
| About 5–10 mA | "Let-go" threshold: above this, many people cannot release a gripped conductor, because the current contracts the hand muscles |
| Tens of mA | Breathing difficulty; strong muscle contraction |
| About 30–50 mA and above, for long enough | Ventricular fibrillation becomes possible: the heart quivers instead of pumping. This is the main cause of death by electric shock |
| Amperes | Severe burns, internal tissue damage, cardiac arrest |

**Duration matters.** The fibrillation threshold falls as the shock lasts longer, especially if it spans a full heartbeat (about one second). This is why protective devices must disconnect *quickly*.

### Body resistance

The current depends on the body's resistance, which is mostly set by the **skin**.

- **Dry, intact skin** can have tens of thousands of ohms or more.
- **Wet, sweaty or broken skin, or a large contact area,** can bring the total down to about **1000 Ω or less**.
- **The body's interior** is only about 500 Ω.
- **Skin breaks down at higher voltages.** Above roughly 50–100 V its resistance collapses, which makes mains voltage especially dangerous.

Using Ohm's law with a typical 1000 Ω (wet conditions):

- 230 V → I = 230 / 1000 = 230 mA, far above the fibrillation range;
- 50 V → 50 mA;
- 12 V → 12 mA in very wet conditions. That is why even extra-low voltages are restricted in wet locations such as bathrooms and swimming pools.

### Current path

- A path **across the chest** (hand to hand, or left hand to feet) passes near the heart and is the most dangerous.
- A path through one hand only (for example, two fingers of the same hand) is less likely to affect the heart, but can still cause deep burns.

### DC versus AC

For the same current, DC is generally less likely than 50/60 Hz AC to cause fibrillation, and it has no clear "let-go" threshold. But:

- DC causes strong muscle contraction when it starts and stops.
- DC arcs are harder to extinguish (lesson L3).
- High-voltage DC, such as the 360–800 V buses in solar and high-frequency inverters, is lethal.

### Why 30 mA RCDs?

A residual current device ([[rcd]]) measures the difference between the current going out on the line and returning on the neutral. Any difference must be leaking somewhere, possibly through a person. A 30 mA RCD trips when the leakage reaches 30 mA, typically within tens of milliseconds at larger leakage currents. That is below the range where fibrillation is likely for such short durations. It is a powerful extra layer of protection, but **not a licence to work live**: it cannot protect against a shock between line and neutral, because no current leaks to earth.

### After a shock

Anyone who has had a significant shock should get medical attention, even if they feel fine: heart rhythm problems and internal burns can appear later. Never touch a person who is still in contact with a live conductor. Isolate the supply first.`,technical:`**Why the heart is vulnerable.** The heart's muscle cells contract in a coordinated wave triggered by an electrical signal. During one part of each heartbeat (the "vulnerable period" of the T-wave) a stimulus can disrupt this wave and start fibrillation. A shock lasting longer than one heartbeat is therefore much more dangerous than a very short one.

**Body impedance is not a simple resistor.** The skin behaves partly like a capacitor in parallel with a resistor, so its impedance falls at higher frequencies and higher voltages. The total body impedance for a large-area, hand-to-hand contact at 230 V is commonly quoted around 1 kΩ (published 50th-percentile values). That is the figure behind the typical 1000 Ω used in teaching estimates.

**Touch voltage limits.** Electrical installation standards limit how long a fault may leave a dangerous [[touch-voltage]] on exposed metal. For ordinary dry conditions the conventional limit is 50 V AC, and lower in wet conditions. That is why earthed metalwork combined with fast disconnection is the basis of shock protection (lesson F1.M4.L2).`,diagrams:[{id:`body-current`,caption:`Body current versus voltage for dry and wet contact, compared with the published threshold ranges.`}],formulas:[{name:`Body current estimate`,expr:`I_body ≈ V_touch / R_body`,where:[[`V_touch`,`voltage across the body`,`V`],[`R_body`,`total body resistance (≈ 1000 Ω wet, much higher dry)`,`Ω`]],note:`An order-of-magnitude estimate. Real body impedance varies with voltage, frequency, path and person.`}],examples:[{title:`Mains contact with wet hands`,problem:`Estimate the current through a person touching 230 V with wet hands (R_body ≈ 1000 Ω). Compare it with the fibrillation range.`,steps:[`I = 230 / 1000 = 0.23 A = 230 mA`,`The fibrillation range starts around 30–50 mA for long durations`],answer:`About 230 mA: several times the level at which fibrillation becomes possible. Mains contact in wet conditions is potentially lethal.`},{title:`Dry contact at 48 V DC`,problem:`A person touches both terminals of a 48 V battery with dry hands (R_body ≈ 20 kΩ). Estimate the current.`,steps:[`Ohm’s law: I = V / R_body`,`I = 48 / 20 000 = 0.0024 A = 2.4 mA, above the perception threshold but below the let-go range for dry skin`],answer:`About 2.4 mA: probably felt, possibly painful, below the let-go range. With wet or damaged skin the current could be ten or twenty times larger, so 48 V systems still need insulated terminals and care.`}],applications:`These numbers explain the rules used everywhere:

- 30 mA RCDs on socket circuits.
- Fast disconnection times for faults.
- Lower extra-low-voltage limits in wet locations.
- Insulated tools.
- The rule that rescuers isolate the supply before touching a casualty.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`“There is a safe current, say 10 mA, so I can work near it.”`,correction:`Thresholds are approximate and vary by person, path and duration. Never plan to receive any shock: isolate instead.`},{mistake:`“An RCD makes live work safe.”`,correction:`An RCD does not trip for a shock between line and neutral (no earth leakage). It is additional protection, not permission.`},{mistake:`Grabbing someone who is being electrocuted.`,correction:`You become part of the circuit. Isolate the supply first, or move them with a dry non-conductive object only if isolation is impossible.`}],demo:{title:`Body current explorer`,text:`Change the voltage and the contact condition (dry, damp, wet) and read the estimated body current against the threshold bands.`,link:e.lib(`Protective earth and RCD protection`,`protective-earth`),embed:`body-current`},activities:[e.cmp(`Fuse versus circuit breaker`,`fuse-vs-breaker`),e.lib(`Circuit breaker`,`circuit-breaker`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Estimated body current at 120 V with R_body = 1000 Ω (mA)?`,answer:120,tol:.01,unit:`mA`,explain:`120/1000 = 0.12 A = 120 mA.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What is the main cause of death by electric shock?`,options:[`Ventricular fibrillation of the heart`,`Hair loss`,`The tingling sensation`,`Static electricity`],answer:0,explain:`Fibrillation stops the heart pumping blood.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which condition makes a shock more dangerous?`,options:[`Wet skin and a hand-to-hand path across the chest`,`Dry skin and a short contact`,`Rubber-soled shoes`,`A low voltage`],answer:0,explain:`Wet skin lowers resistance; the chest path passes near the heart.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A 30 mA RCD will NOT protect against…`,options:[`a shock between line and neutral`,`a shock from line to earth`,`leakage through a damaged cable to earth`,`a faulty appliance case touching line`],answer:0,explain:`Line-to-neutral current returns on the neutral, so there is no residual current to detect.`}],summary:[`Approximate published ranges: perception ~0.5 mA, let-go ~5–10 mA, fibrillation possible from ~30–50 mA for long durations.`,`Body resistance depends on skin condition: high when dry, ~1000 Ω or less when wet; it falls at higher voltage.`,`Paths across the chest and longer durations are the most dangerous.`,`30 mA RCDs disconnect earth-leakage shocks quickly, but not line-to-neutral shocks.`,`Isolate before touching a casualty; seek medical attention after any significant shock.`],homework:{id:`F1.M1.L2.hw`,title:`Shock estimates`,brief:`Estimate body currents with I = V/R.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`230 V, dry contact R = 50 kΩ: current (mA)?`,answer:4.6,tol:.02,unit:`mA`,explain:`230/50 000 = 4.6 mA.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`230 V, wet contact R = 800 Ω: current (mA)?`,answer:287.5,tol:.02,unit:`mA`,explain:`230/800 = 287.5 mA.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Voltage that drives 30 mA through 1000 Ω (V)?`,answer:30,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`V = 0.03 × 1000 = 30 V.`}],written:[{prompt:`Why are extra-low-voltage limits lower in bathrooms and around swimming pools?`,model:`Wet skin and immersion greatly reduce body resistance, so a lower voltage can drive a dangerous current; standards therefore require lower voltages (or additional protection) in these locations.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M1.L3`,title:`Shock, arc flash, burns and fire`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Distinguish the four main electrical injuries: shock, arc flash, burns and secondary injuries.`,`Explain how overloads, short circuits and poor connections start fires.`,`Calculate the heat produced in a conductor or connection with P = I²R.`,`Explain why DC arcs are particularly hard to extinguish.`],introduction:`Shock is only one way electricity injures people. Many electrical injuries are **burns**:

- from an arc;
- from touching an overheated conductor;
- from molten metal sprayed by a short circuit.

Electrical **fires** destroy buildings and kill people who were never near the faulty equipment. And a mild shock can cause a fall from a ladder. This lesson covers these hazards, all of which apply with full force to battery and inverter systems.`,lecture:`### Arc flash

An electric arc forms when current jumps through the air between conductors:

- when a short circuit blows apart;
- when a connector carrying current is pulled apart;
- when a dropped tool bridges two terminals.

The arc is a column of ionised gas at thousands of degrees. It releases intense heat and ultraviolet light, can vaporise copper (which expands violently), and throws molten metal. An [[arc-flash|arc flash]] can cause severe burns and eye damage at a distance of a metre or more, *without touching anything*.

**Battery banks and DC buses can sustain powerful arcs.** A battery can supply very large currents, and DC has no zero-crossing to help the arc go out.

### Why DC arcs are stubborn

An AC current passes through zero 100 or 120 times a second, and an arc tends to go out at each zero. DC never passes through zero, so a DC arc, once established, can keep burning as long as the voltage can sustain it. This is why:

- DC switches, fuses and breakers must be specifically rated for DC at the system voltage. An AC-only switch may fail to interrupt DC.
- PV and battery connectors must never be disconnected under load.

### Burns from heating: P = I²R

Every resistance carrying current heats up, with power P = I²R.

- **Overload:** more current than a cable is rated for. Twice the current gives four times the heat. The insulation softens, ages, and eventually burns.
- **Short circuit:** a near-zero-resistance fault draws enormous current. Wires glow and melt within seconds unless a fuse or breaker opens.
- **Poor connections:** a loose or corroded terminal adds a small resistance exactly where cooling is poor. A few milliohms carrying 100 A can produce tens of watts in one small spot, enough to char plastic and start a fire. Loose connections are a leading cause of electrical fires in battery systems.

### Fire

Electrical fires start where heat builds up faster than it escapes: overloaded cables, loose connections, failed components, overheated batteries. Prevention means:

- correctly sized cables;
- protective devices (fuses and breakers sized for the cable);
- tight, correct connections (torque to the manufacturer's value);
- ventilation;
- keeping combustible materials away from equipment.

> **Electrical fires:** isolate the supply if it can be done safely. Never use water on energised equipment. Use an extinguisher rated for electrical fires (typically CO₂ or dry powder), and follow local emergency procedures. Lithium battery fires need special handling.

### Secondary injuries

A mild shock that is not harmful in itself can make someone jump back, drop a tool, or fall from a ladder. Many electrical accidents cause injury this way. Working on a stable platform, keeping tools secure and isolating first prevent them.`,technical:`**Energy in a fault.** The heating of a conductor during a short circuit depends on I²t: the square of the current multiplied by its duration. Fuses and breakers are rated by how quickly they limit I²t. Cables have a matching maximum I²t that their insulation can withstand. Coordinating the two is part of protection design (F1.M4.L1).

**Arc flash in industry.** Industrial installations calculate the "incident energy" an arc could deliver at a working distance. They set boundaries and arc-rated PPE accordingly, using standardised methods. That is specialist work beyond this course; the point here is that arcs injure at a distance.

**Lithium-ion batteries.** Damage, overcharge or internal faults can trigger [[thermal-runaway|thermal runaway]], in which cells heat themselves, vent flammable and toxic gases and can catch fire. LiFePO₄ chemistry is less prone to this than other lithium-ion chemistries, but no battery is risk-free (course P5).`,diagrams:[{id:`circuit-loop`,caption:`Where heat appears in a circuit: the load, the cables and every connection carry the same current and heat by I²R.`}],formulas:[{name:`Heating in a resistance`,expr:`P = I² · R`,where:[[`P`,`heat produced`,`W`],[`I`,`current`,`A`],[`R`,`resistance`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Energy over time`,expr:`E = P · t`,where:[[`E`,`energy`,`J`],[`t`,`time`,`s`]]}],examples:[{title:`A loose battery terminal`,problem:`A corroded terminal has a resistance of 5 mΩ and carries 100 A. How much heat is produced in it?`,steps:[`Heat in a resistance: P = I²R`,`P = 100² × 0.005 = 10 000 × 0.005 = 50 W, concentrated in one small joint`],answer:`50 W concentrated in a small terminal: like a soldering iron, enough to melt plastic and start a fire. A good connection (0.1 mΩ) would produce only 1 W.`},{title:`Overloading a cable`,problem:`A cable produces 10 W of heat at its rated current. How much does it produce at 1.5 × and 2 × the rated current?`,steps:[`Heat ∝ I²`,`1.5×: 10 × 1.5² = 22.5 W`,`2×: 10 × 2² = 40 W`],answer:`22.5 W and 40 W. Doubling the current quadruples the heat, which is why overload protection matters.`}],applications:`Battery installation manuals specify terminal torques, cable sizes and fuse ratings for exactly these reasons. Thermal imaging cameras find loose connections by their heat before they fail (course S5). DC isolators and fuses are rated for DC breaking.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using an AC-rated switch or fuse in a battery or PV DC circuit.`,correction:`DC arcs do not self-extinguish; use devices rated for DC at the system voltage.`},{mistake:`Pulling a PV or battery connector apart under load.`,correction:`This draws an arc. Switch off the load and isolate first.`},{mistake:`Using water on an electrical fire.`,correction:`Water conducts. Isolate if safe and use an extinguisher rated for electrical fires.`}],demo:{title:`Heat from a bad connection`,text:`Use the Ohm’s-law explorer in power mode: hold the current at 100 A and vary the connection resistance from 0.1 mΩ to 10 mΩ, watching the heat produced.`,link:e.lib(`Battery terminals`,`battery-terminals`),embed:`ohms-law`},activities:[e.lib(`Fuse: breaking capacity and DC ratings`,`fuse`),e.lib(`Battery terminals`,`battery-terminals`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Heat in a 2 mΩ connection carrying 150 A (W)?`,answer:45,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`150² × 0.002 = 45 W.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why are DC arcs harder to extinguish than AC arcs?`,options:[`DC current never passes through zero`,`DC is always higher voltage`,`DC arcs are colder`,`They are not`],answer:0,explain:`AC arcs tend to extinguish at each current zero.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`If the current in a cable doubles, its heating…`,options:[`quadruples`,`doubles`,`halves`,`stays the same`],answer:0,explain:`P = I²R.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An arc flash can injure…`,options:[`a person standing nearby without touching anything`,`only the person touching the conductor`,`nobody if the voltage is DC`,`only in wet conditions`],answer:0,explain:`Heat, light and molten metal reach people at a distance.`}],summary:[`Electrical injuries include shock, arc-flash burns, contact burns and secondary injuries such as falls.`,`Heat in any resistance is P = I²R: overloads, shorts and loose connections cause fires.`,`DC arcs do not self-extinguish; use DC-rated devices and never break DC circuits under load.`,`Prevent fires with correct cable sizes, protection, tight connections and ventilation.`,`Never use water on electrical fires; isolate if safe.`],homework:{id:`F1.M1.L3.hw`,title:`Heating calculations`,brief:`Use P = I²R and E = P·t.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Heat in a 0.5 mΩ fuse holder carrying 80 A (W)?`,answer:3.2,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`80² × 0.0005 = 3.2 W.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy released by 50 W over 1 hour (kJ)?`,answer:180,tol:.01,unit:`kJ`,explain:`50 × 3600 = 180 000 J = 180 kJ.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A connection heats 4 W at 40 A. Heat at 60 A (W)?`,answer:9,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`4 × (60/40)² = 9 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`List three precautions that prevent a battery terminal from becoming a fire hazard.`,model:`Tighten to the manufacturer’s torque with correct, clean lugs; use correctly sized cable with a fuse rated for the cable; inspect periodically for corrosion or heat discoloration (and ventilate the battery area).`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current drawn by a 23 Ω heater on 230 V (A)?`,answer:10,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`230/23 = 10 A.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which quantity causes harm in the body?`,options:[`Current`,`Resistance`,`Frequency`,`Insulation`],answer:0,explain:`Current through the body causes the effects; voltage drives it.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The approximate let-go threshold for AC is…`,options:[`about 5–10 mA`,`about 0.5 mA`,`about 1 A`,`about 100 A`],answer:0,explain:`Published ranges place it around 5–10 mA.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Estimated body current at 230 V with 2300 Ω (mA)?`,answer:100,tol:.01,unit:`mA`,explain:`230/2300 = 0.1 A.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why must fault protection disconnect quickly?`,options:[`The fibrillation threshold falls as shock duration increases`,`To save electricity`,`Because of the meter`,`It need not`],answer:0,explain:`Duration strongly affects danger.`},{id:`q6`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Heat in a 4 mΩ joint at 50 A (W)?`,answer:10,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`50² × 0.004 = 10 W.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which device is required to interrupt a battery DC circuit safely?`,options:[`A switch or fuse rated for DC at the system voltage`,`Any AC light switch`,`A plug pulled under load`,`A non-contact tester`],answer:0,explain:`DC arcs need DC-rated devices.`},{id:`q8`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which can injure without touching a live conductor? (select all)`,options:[`Arc flash`,`Molten metal from a short circuit`,`A fall caused by a mild shock`,`Reading a meter display`],answers:[0,1,2],explain:`Arcs, molten metal and secondary injuries all harm without direct contact.`}]},assignment:{id:`F1.M1.asg`,title:`Module assignment: hazard analysis of a battery workbench`,brief:`A 24 V battery bank (internal resistance 20 mΩ) is on a workbench with a 230 V soldering station plugged in nearby.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Prospective short-circuit current of the battery through a 5 mΩ spanner (A)?`,answer:960,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`24/(0.020 + 0.005) = 960 A.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Heat in the spanner (kW)?`,answer:4.608,tol:.02,unit:`kW`,explain:`960² × 0.005 = 4608 W.`},{id:`a3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Body current if a wet hand (1000 Ω) touches 230 V (mA)?`,answer:230,tol:.01,unit:`mA`,explain:`230/1000 = 230 mA.`}],written:[{prompt:`Name the two different hazards on this bench and one control for each.`,model:`Battery short circuit (burns, arc, fire): insulated tools, remove jewellery, terminal covers, a fuse at the battery. Mains shock from the soldering station: an RCD-protected socket, an undamaged cable, keeping it away from water.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F1.M1.P1`,title:`Identify the hazards on a workbench`,description:`Find every electrical hazard in a described workshop scene, and only the hazards.`,task:{id:`f1-hazards-bench`,kind:`hazards`,title:`Workbench hazard identification`,brief:`Read the description of the workbench, then select every item that is an electrical hazard. Items that are good practice must not be selected.`,scene:`A technician is preparing to replace a fuse in a 12 V inverter connected to a 100 Ah battery. The bench has: a spanner lying across the battery terminals area; the technician wearing a metal watch; a battery with plastic terminal covers fitted on the negative but not the positive; a puddle of spilled coffee next to a mains extension lead; a voltage tester and its proving unit; an extension lead whose outer sheath is cracked, showing the coloured inner wires; a fire extinguisher rated for electrical fires on the wall; and the inverter still switched on with the battery connected.`,items:[{id:`spanner`,label:`Spanner lying near the battery terminals`,hazard:!0,why:`It could bridge the terminals and short the battery: hundreds of amperes, burns and fire.`},{id:`watch`,label:`Metal watch on the wrist`,hazard:!0,why:`Metal jewellery can bridge terminals and heat instantly. Remove rings, watches and bracelets.`},{id:`cover`,label:`Positive terminal uncovered`,hazard:!0,why:`The exposed positive terminal can be shorted to the frame or a tool; covers should be on both.`},{id:`liquid`,label:`Spilled liquid beside the mains extension lead`,hazard:!0,why:`Liquid near mains lowers resistance and can cause shock or tracking.`},{id:`tester`,label:`Voltage tester with proving unit`,hazard:!1,why:`Good practice: needed to prove dead before work.`},{id:`sheath`,label:`Cracked extension-lead sheath`,hazard:!0,why:`Damaged insulation exposes conductors to touch and mechanical damage. Take the lead out of use.`},{id:`extinguisher`,label:`Electrical-rated fire extinguisher`,hazard:!1,why:`Good practice: correct extinguisher available.`},{id:`live`,label:`Inverter on and battery still connected while replacing a fuse`,hazard:!0,why:`The work must be done isolated: switch off, disconnect the battery and prove dead first.`}],explain:`Hazards are anything that could create a short circuit, a shock path or an arc, or that leaves equipment energised during work. The tester and the extinguisher are controls, not hazards.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f1-hazards-bench`}}]},{id:`F1.M2`,title:`Energy sources and stored energy`,summary:`The sources of energy in inverter and solar systems — batteries, capacitors, inductors, PV arrays, the grid and generators — and why each stays dangerous after an obvious switch has been turned off.`,lessons:[{id:`F1.M2.L1`,title:`Batteries: short-circuit current, gases and chemicals`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Calculate the prospective short-circuit current of a battery from its voltage and internal resistance.`,`Explain the hydrogen, acid and thermal-runaway hazards of lead-acid and lithium batteries.`,`State the safe-handling rules for battery work.`,`Explain why a battery cannot be “switched off”.`],introduction:`A battery is a store of chemical energy that is always ready to deliver it. There is no switch inside a battery, and it cannot be made dead: its terminals are live as long as it exists. Battery banks in inverter and solar systems hold kilowatt-hours of energy and can deliver thousands of amperes into a fault. Battery safety is therefore the first practical skill of this programme.`,lecture:`### A battery as a source with internal resistance

A battery behaves like an ideal voltage source (its [[emf|EMF]]) in series with a small [[internal-resistance|internal resistance]] R_int. When something connects its terminals, the current is

I = V / (R_int + R_external)

With a short circuit (R_external ≈ 0), only R_int limits the current. Healthy batteries have very low internal resistance, which is exactly what makes them good at delivering power.

| Battery (typical values; see the datasheet) | Internal resistance | Prospective short-circuit current |
|---|---|---|
| 12 V, 100 Ah lead-acid | about 5–10 mΩ | about 1.2–2.4 kA |
| 12.8 V, 100 Ah LiFePO₄ | about 2–5 mΩ | a few kA |
| 48 V bank of four 12 V batteries in series | about 4 × the single-battery value | similar order (V and R both scale ×4) |

### Burns, arcs and jewellery

A spanner or a ring across battery terminals carries that current. The metal heats to glowing in seconds, the arc throws molten metal, and the battery can be damaged or explode. **Remove rings, watches and metal bracelets.** Use insulated tools. Keep terminal covers on, and never lay tools on a battery.

### The fuse belongs at the battery

Because a battery cannot be switched off, the cable from it must be protected by a [[fuse]] (or DC breaker) placed **as close to the battery terminal as possible**. That way every metre of cable after it is protected. A short on the unfused stretch between battery and fuse is limited only by R_int.

### Gases and chemicals

**Lead-acid batteries:**
- Vented ("flooded") types release **hydrogen and oxygen** when charged, especially near full charge. Hydrogen is explosive in air between about 4 % and 75 %.
- Battery rooms and enclosures must be **ventilated**. Sparks and flames must be kept away, including the spark from connecting or disconnecting a terminal.
- The electrolyte is **sulphuric acid**: wear eye protection and have water available for flushing.
- Sealed (VRLA, AGM, gel) types release less gas, but can vent if overcharged.

**Lithium batteries:**
- Damage, overcharging, short circuits or internal defects can cause [[thermal-runaway|thermal runaway]]: rapid heating, venting of flammable and toxic gases, and fire.
- Lithium batteries must be used with a battery management system (BMS) that protects the cells.

### Safe handling rules

1. Wear eye protection; remove metal jewellery; use insulated tools.
2. Ventilate; no sparks or flames near vented lead-acid batteries.
3. Fit terminal covers; never lay tools on a battery.
4. Disconnect the **negative** (earthed) lead first and reconnect it last. Then a slip of the spanner from the positive terminal to the earthed frame cannot cause a short.
5. Fuse at the battery; use cable rated for the fuse.
6. Lift heavy batteries correctly. A 100 Ah lead-acid battery weighs around 25–30 kg (typical).`,technical:`**Why “disconnect negative first”?** In most vehicle and many DC systems, the negative terminal is connected to the metal frame. While you loosen the positive terminal, your spanner may touch the frame. If the negative is still connected, that completes a short through the spanner. If the negative is already disconnected, there is no return path. The reverse order applies when reconnecting.

**Series and parallel banks.** In series, voltages add (four 12 V batteries make 48 V) and internal resistances add. In parallel, capacities add and the internal resistance falls, so the short-circuit current *increases*. Course F2 covers battery banks in detail, and course P5 covers battery engineering.

**Ventilation calculations.** Standards for stationary batteries give formulas for the required airflow from the charging current and the number of cells. That is specialist installation work (course S3).`,diagrams:[{id:`battery-model`,caption:`A battery as an EMF in series with its internal resistance. The short-circuit current is limited only by R_int.`}],formulas:[{name:`Prospective short-circuit current`,expr:`I_sc = V / (R_int + R_fault)`,where:[[`V`,`battery EMF`,`V`],[`R_int`,`internal resistance`,`Ω`],[`R_fault`,`resistance of the fault path`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Stored energy of a battery`,expr:`E = V · C_Ah`,where:[[`E`,`energy`,`Wh`],[`C_Ah`,`capacity`,`Ah`]]}],examples:[{title:`Short-circuit current of a lead-acid battery`,problem:`A 12 V battery has R_int = 8 mΩ. A tool of 2 mΩ shorts its terminals. Find the current and the power in the tool.`,steps:[`I = 12 / (0.008 + 0.002) = 12 / 0.010 = 1200 A`,`P_tool = I²R = 1200² × 0.002 = 2880 W`],answer:`1200 A and nearly 3 kW in the tool: it glows and melts within seconds.`},{title:`Energy in a battery bank`,problem:`How much energy is stored in a 48 V, 200 Ah battery bank? How long could it supply a 2 kW load (ignoring losses and depth-of-discharge limits)?`,steps:[`E = 48 × 200 = 9600 Wh = 9.6 kWh`,`t = 9600 / 2000 = 4.8 h`],answer:`9.6 kWh, enough for 4.8 hours at 2 kW. That energy can be released in a fault in a fraction of a second.`}],applications:`Battery installation instructions require all of these measures: fuses or breakers at the battery, terminal covers, ventilation of battery rooms and enclosures, eye-wash stations in larger installations, and BMS protection for lithium systems.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Placing the main fuse next to the inverter, two metres from the battery.`,correction:`The cable between battery and fuse is then unprotected. Fit the fuse at the battery.`},{mistake:`Disconnecting the positive terminal first.`,correction:`Disconnect the earthed negative first, so a spanner touching the frame cannot complete a short.`},{mistake:`Charging a vented lead-acid battery in a closed cupboard.`,correction:`Hydrogen accumulates; ventilate and keep sparks away.`}],demo:{title:`Battery under load and in a fault`,text:`Change the load resistance on a battery model from normal loads down to a short circuit, and watch the terminal voltage, the current and the heat in the internal resistance.`,link:e.lib(`Battery terminals`,`battery-terminals`),embed:`battery-load`},activities:[e.lib(`Battery terminals`,`battery-terminals`),e.lib(`Fuse`,`fuse`),e.lab3d(`LF-3K battery terminals`,`lf3k`,`bat-pos`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Short-circuit current of a 24 V battery with R_int = 12 mΩ and a 3 mΩ fault (A)?`,answer:1600,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`24/0.015 = 1600 A.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which terminal should be disconnected first in a system with an earthed negative?`,options:[`Negative`,`Positive`,`Either`,`Both at once`],answer:0,explain:`Removing the earthed negative removes the return path for an accidental short.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which gas is released by vented lead-acid batteries during charging?`,options:[`Hydrogen`,`Carbon monoxide`,`Chlorine`,`Helium`],answer:0,explain:`Electrolysis of the electrolyte releases hydrogen and oxygen.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy stored in a 12 V, 150 Ah battery (Wh)?`,answer:1800,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`12 × 150 = 1800 Wh.`}],summary:[`A battery cannot be switched off; its terminals are always live.`,`Short-circuit current = V/(R_int + R_fault): often thousands of amperes.`,`Fuse at the battery; remove jewellery; use insulated tools; keep terminal covers on.`,`Lead-acid releases explosive hydrogen and contains acid; lithium can enter thermal runaway.`,`Disconnect the earthed negative first and reconnect it last.`],homework:{id:`F1.M2.L1.hw`,title:`Battery hazards`,brief:`A 48 V bank of four 12 V batteries in series, each with R_int = 6 mΩ.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Total internal resistance (mΩ)?`,answer:24,tol:.01,unit:`mΩ`,explain:`4 × 6 = 24 mΩ.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Prospective short-circuit current at the bank terminals (A)?`,answer:2e3,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`48/0.024 = 2000 A.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Two such banks in parallel: short-circuit current (A)?`,answer:4e3,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`R halves to 12 mΩ: 48/0.012 = 4000 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`Write the safe sequence for disconnecting a battery from an inverter for maintenance.`,model:`Switch the inverter and loads off; open the battery fuse or isolator; remove jewellery and wear eye protection; with insulated tools, disconnect the negative lead first, then the positive; fit terminal covers; prove dead on the inverter side before working on it.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M2.L2`,title:`Capacitors and inductors: stored energy`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Calculate the energy stored in a capacitor and in an inductor.`,`Calculate the time a capacitor takes to discharge through a resistor.`,`Explain why opening an inductive circuit causes a high-voltage spike.`,`Explain why equipment can stay dangerous after it is switched off.`],introduction:`Switching off a supply stops new energy arriving. It does not remove energy already stored inside the equipment. Inverters contain large capacitor banks that can hold a lethal charge for minutes after switch-off. They also contain inductors and transformers that release their stored energy as a violent voltage spike when their current is interrupted.`,lecture:`### Capacitors

A capacitor stores energy in the electric field between two plates. Its stored energy is

E = ½ · C · V²

where C is the capacitance in farads and V is the voltage. Because the energy rises with V², high-voltage capacitors are especially dangerous.

| Capacitor (typical inverter values) | Voltage | Stored energy |
|---|---|---|
| 4700 µF battery-bus capacitor | 28.8 V | about 2 J: a spark hazard |
| 470 µF HV bus capacitor | 400 V | about 38 J: a dangerous shock |
| 1000 µF photo-flash type | 330 V | about 54 J |

A capacitor stays charged after the supply is removed, unless something discharges it.

### Discharge through a resistor

Connected to a resistor R, a capacitor's voltage decays exponentially:

v(t) = V₀ · e^(−t / RC)

The product RC is the [[time-constant|time constant]] τ. After one τ the voltage is 37 % of V₀; after 5τ it is below 1 %. The time to reach a safe voltage V_s is

t = RC · ln(V₀ / V_s)

Equipment is fitted with [[bleeder]] resistors that discharge the capacitors after switch-off. But bleeders can fail, and the waiting time may be minutes, so the rule is: **wait, then verify with a meter** (lesson F1.M3.L3).

### Inductors

An inductor, such as a coil, a transformer winding or a motor, stores energy in its magnetic field:

E = ½ · L · I²

An inductor resists any change in its current. If you suddenly open a circuit carrying current through an inductor, the inductor generates whatever voltage it takes to keep the current flowing for an instant: V = L · di/dt. Interrupting current quickly can therefore produce hundreds or thousands of volts, enough to:

- jump across switch contacts as an arc;
- destroy semiconductors;
- give a shock even from a low-voltage circuit.

This is why relay coils have diodes across them, and why inverter switches have snubbers and clamps. It is also why you must never disconnect a current-carrying coil by hand.

### Practical consequences

- An inverter switched off seconds ago may still have charged bus capacitors.
- A high-frequency or solar inverter's DC bus (several hundred volts) is the most dangerous stored-energy source in the system.
- Large motors, transformers and long cables can also hold or generate energy after disconnection.`,technical:`**Why ln appears.** The discharge current i = v/R removes charge at a rate proportional to the voltage itself, which gives the exponential solution. Taking logarithms of v(t) = V₀ e^(−t/τ) gives t = τ ln(V₀/v).

**Dielectric absorption.** Large electrolytic capacitors can partly recharge themselves after being discharged ("soakage"), as charge trapped in the dielectric returns. A capacitor discharged to zero can read several volts a few minutes later. That is one reason why large capacitors are stored with shorting links, and why voltage is re-checked before work.

**Spike magnitude.** If 2 A in a 0.5 H relay coil is interrupted in 10 µs, L·di/dt = 0.5 × 2 / 10⁻⁵ = 100 000 V in theory. In practice the voltage rises until something breaks down — an arc across the contacts, or a semiconductor — which is exactly the damage engineers design against.`,diagrams:[{id:`rc-curve`,caption:`Capacitor discharge through a bleeder: after each time constant the voltage falls to 37 % of its previous value.`}],formulas:[{name:`Energy in a capacitor`,expr:`E = ½ · C · V²`,where:[[`C`,`capacitance`,`F`],[`V`,`voltage`,`V`],[`E`,`energy`,`J`]]},{name:`Discharge time`,expr:`t = R · C · ln(V₀ / V_s)`,where:[[`R`,`discharge resistance`,`Ω`],[`V₀`,`initial voltage`,`V`],[`V_s`,`safe voltage`,`V`]]},{name:`Energy in an inductor`,expr:`E = ½ · L · I²`,where:[[`L`,`inductance`,`H`],[`I`,`current`,`A`]]},{name:`Inductive voltage`,expr:`V = L · di/dt`,where:[[`di/dt`,`rate of change of current`,`A/s`]]}],examples:[{title:`Energy and waiting time for an HV bus`,problem:`A 330 µF bus capacitor is charged to 380 V and has a 220 kΩ bleeder. Find its energy and the time to fall below 50 V.`,steps:[`E = ½ × 330 × 10⁻⁶ × 380² = ½ × 3.3 × 10⁻⁴ × 144 400 = 23.8 J`,`τ = 220 000 × 330 × 10⁻⁶ = 72.6 s`,`t = 72.6 × ln(380/50) = 72.6 × 2.028 = 147 s`],answer:`23.8 J, and about 2½ minutes to fall below 50 V, if the bleeder is working.`},{title:`Energy in an inductor`,problem:`An output inductor of 2 mH carries 20 A. How much energy does it store?`,steps:[`Inductor energy: E = ½ · L · I²`,`E = ½ × 0.002 × 20² = ½ × 0.002 × 400 = 0.4 J`],answer:`0.4 J: small, but released in microseconds when the current is interrupted, so the resulting voltage spike can still destroy a switch.`}],applications:`Inverter service manuals state the capacitor discharge time and require the bus voltage to be measured before work. Relay coils carry flyback diodes. Snubber circuits protect inverter switches from inductive spikes. The 3D laboratory's guided procedure includes a verified discharge before any internal part may be touched.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`“The display went dark, so the capacitors are empty.”`,correction:`Displays turn off at a much higher voltage than is safe. Measure the bus voltage.`},{mistake:`Shorting a large capacitor with a screwdriver to discharge it.`,correction:`This causes a violent arc, can weld the tool and damage the capacitor. Use a rated discharge resistor and verify with a meter.`},{mistake:`Assuming low-voltage circuits with coils are harmless to interrupt.`,correction:`Interrupting inductor current produces high-voltage spikes.`}],demo:{title:`Capacitor discharge timer`,text:`Choose a capacitance, an initial voltage and a bleeder resistance, and watch the voltage decay. Read off the stored energy and the time to reach 50 V.`,link:e.lab3d(`LF-3K bus capacitors`,`lf3k`,`c1`),embed:`cap-discharge`},activities:[e.lib(`Capacitor`,`capacitor`),e.lib(`Inductor`,`inductor`),e.lab3d(`High-voltage bus capacitors`,`lf3k`,`c5`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy in 2200 µF at 60 V (J)?`,answer:3.96,tol:.01,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.0022 × 3600 = 3.96 J.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Time constant of 470 µF and 100 kΩ (s)?`,answer:47,tol:.01,unit:`s`,explain:`τ = RC = 47 s.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`After 5 time constants, a discharging capacitor has…`,options:[`less than 1 % of its initial voltage`,`50 % of its voltage`,`37 % of its voltage`,`no change`],answer:0,explain:`e⁻⁵ ≈ 0.7 %.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Suddenly opening a circuit carrying current through a coil causes…`,options:[`a high-voltage spike`,`the current to vanish harmlessly`,`the coil to cool`,`a lower voltage`],answer:0,explain:`V = L·di/dt becomes very large.`}],summary:[`Capacitors store E = ½CV²; high-voltage buses can hold tens of joules for minutes.`,`Discharge follows v = V₀·e^(−t/RC); t = RC·ln(V₀/V_s).`,`Bleeders can fail: wait, then measure.`,`Inductors store E = ½LI² and produce V = L·di/dt spikes when current is interrupted.`,`Switching off removes the source, not the stored energy.`],homework:{id:`F1.M2.L2.hw`,title:`Stored energy calculations`,brief:`Use E = ½CV², E = ½LI² and t = RC·ln(V₀/V_s).`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy in 680 µF at 400 V (J)?`,answer:54.4,tol:.01,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 680 × 10⁻⁶ × 160 000 = 54.4 J.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Time for it to fall to 50 V through 150 kΩ (s)?`,answer:212.1,tol:.02,unit:`s`,explain:`τ = 102 s; t = 102 × ln(8) = 102 × 2.079 = 212 s.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy in a 10 mH inductor at 15 A (J)?`,answer:1.125,tol:.01,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.01 × 225 = 1.125 J.`}],written:[{prompt:`Why is it not enough to wait the calculated discharge time before touching a capacitor bank?`,model:`The bleeder may have failed (open circuit), leaving the bank charged indefinitely; dielectric absorption can also cause recovery voltage. Only a measurement with a verified meter proves the bank is discharged.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M2.L3`,title:`Multiple sources: PV arrays, grid, generators and back-feed`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Identify every source of energy in a solar, battery and inverter installation.`,`Explain why a PV array cannot be switched off by daylight and how it is isolated.`,`Explain back-feed from inverters, UPS units, generators and shared neutrals.`,`Apply the rule “isolate every source” before work.`],introduction:`A simple circuit has one source and one switch. Modern energy systems have many sources:

- the grid;
- a generator;
- a battery bank;
- a PV array;
- the inverter itself, which *creates* AC.

Turning off one of them may leave the others energising the circuit you are about to touch. This lesson trains you to find every source.`,lecture:`### Count the sources

In a hybrid solar installation (as in build project H), the sources are:

1. **The grid**, at the AC input.
2. **A generator**, at the AC input, often via a changeover switch.
3. **The battery bank** (DC), which is always live.
4. **The PV array** (DC), live whenever light falls on it.
5. **The inverter output** (AC), generated from the battery or PV even when the grid is off.
6. **Stored energy** in capacitors (lesson L2).

Isolation means making *every one* of these safe for the part you will work on.

### PV arrays cannot be switched off by daylight

A [[pv]] array produces voltage whenever light falls on it, even dim daylight or strong moonlight in some conditions. Its open-circuit voltage ([[voc|V_oc]]) appears even when no current flows, and strings of modules in series reach hundreds of volts DC.

- PV arrays are isolated with **DC isolators** rated for the string voltage and current. Covering the array is not a reliable method.
- PV connectors must not be disconnected under load: a DC arc forms (lesson F1.M1.L3).
- The array's short-circuit current ([[isc|I_sc]]) is only a little above its operating current, so a PV fault may *not* blow a fuse. A PV fault can therefore persist and keep heating.

### Back-feed

[[back-feed|Back-feed]] is energy arriving from an unexpected direction:

- **Inverters and UPS units** produce AC on their outputs from batteries. If an inverter output is wired into a circuit, switching off the grid breaker does not de-energise that circuit.
- **Generators** connected without a proper changeover switch can energise the grid wiring, endangering line workers. That is why grid-tied inverters must have [[anti-islanding]] protection, and generators must connect through a changeover.
- **Borrowed neutrals.** If two circuits share a neutral by mistake, isolating one circuit leaves its neutral carrying the other circuit's current, at a dangerous voltage. That is a [[borrowed-neutral|borrowed neutral]].
- **Capacitors and motors** that are still spinning can generate or hold voltage after isolation.

### The rule

**Identify every source from the drawings and labels, isolate each one, and then prove dead at the point of work.** Never assume that turning off "the main switch" makes everything dead. The proving-dead step (lesson F1.M3.L2) exists precisely because isolation can be incomplete.`,technical:`**Why PV short-circuit current does not blow fuses.** A module's short-circuit current is typically only 5–10 % above its maximum-power current (see the module datasheet). A fault between two points of a string may therefore draw no more than normal current from the healthy parts. PV fuses protect against *reverse current* from parallel strings, not against faults within a string (course S2).

**Islanding.** If the grid fails while a grid-tied inverter keeps exporting, a section of the grid could stay energised (an "island"). Certified grid-tied inverters detect loss of grid and stop within a defined time. That is required by grid codes, not optional.

**Labels and drawings.** Installation standards require warning labels at points of connection showing all sources, such as "dual supply" or "PV system". Reading the labels and the single-line diagram is the first step of isolation (course S3).`,diagrams:[{id:`hybrid-paths`,caption:`Every source in a hybrid system: grid, generator, battery, PV (Phase 7) and the inverter output.`}],formulas:[{name:`PV string open-circuit voltage`,expr:`V_oc,string = N_series × V_oc,module`,where:[[`N_series`,`modules in series`,`—`],[`V_oc,module`,`module open-circuit voltage`,`V`]],note:`V_oc rises in cold weather (course S2).`}],examples:[{title:`PV string voltage`,problem:`A string has 10 modules in series, each with V_oc = 45 V. What voltage is present at the string connectors in daylight with the isolator open?`,steps:[`In series, module voltages add; with the isolator open no current flows, so each module sits at V_oc`,`V_oc,string = 10 × 45 = 450 V DC`],answer:`450 V DC: a lethal voltage that cannot be switched off at the array, only isolated from the rest of the system.`},{title:`Counting the sources`,problem:`A clinic has a grid supply, a diesel generator, a 48 V battery bank, a PV array and a hybrid inverter. You need to replace the inverter’s output breaker. Which sources must be isolated?`,steps:[`Grid and generator: they reach the output through the transfer relay`,`Battery: the inverter generates AC from it`,`PV: it can power the inverter directly`,`Then wait for the capacitors to discharge, and prove dead at the breaker`],answer:`All four sources, plus stored energy. Turning off only the grid would leave the breaker live from the inverter.`}],applications:`Utility workers rely on anti-islanding and changeover switches. Solar installers fit DC isolators and labels at every point. Electricians check for borrowed neutrals when testing circuits. All of these follow from the multiple-source principle.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Covering a PV array with a tarpaulin and treating it as dead.`,correction:`Coverings slip and leak light; isolate with the DC isolator and prove dead.`},{mistake:`Switching off the grid breaker and working on an inverter output circuit.`,correction:`The inverter can back-feed from its battery. Isolate the inverter and the battery too.`},{mistake:`Connecting a generator to a house through a socket (“back-feeding”).`,correction:`This energises the grid wiring and endangers others; use a proper changeover switch.`}],demo:{title:`Energy paths in a hybrid inverter`,text:`Open build project H and trace every path by which energy reaches the load, including those that remain live when the grid is off.`,link:e.build(`Project H: hybrid inverter energy paths`,`bld-h`)},activities:[e.lib(`Solar (PV) input`,`solar-input`),e.lib(`Relay (transfer switching)`,`relay`),e.lab3d(`LF-3K PV input terminals`,`lf3k`,`pv-pos`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Open-circuit voltage of 12 modules with V_oc = 40 V each in series (V)?`,answer:480,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`12 × 40 = 480 V.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`How is a PV array made safe to work on downstream?`,options:[`Open its DC isolator and prove dead downstream`,`Wait for a cloud`,`Switch off the inverter display`,`Remove the grid fuse`],answer:0,explain:`The array keeps generating; isolate it.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What is back-feed?`,options:[`Energy arriving at an isolated circuit from another source`,`A type of fuse`,`A battery charging stage`,`A cable colour`],answer:0,explain:`For example, from an inverter, generator or shared neutral.`},{id:`k4`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which can keep a circuit live after the grid breaker is opened? (select all)`,options:[`An inverter with a battery`,`A PV array feeding an inverter`,`A generator without a changeover`,`An opened DC isolator`],answers:[0,1,2],explain:`An opened isolator is a means of isolation, not a source.`}],summary:[`Energy systems have many sources: grid, generator, battery, PV, inverter output and stored energy.`,`PV arrays are live in daylight; isolate them with rated DC isolators.`,`Back-feed comes from inverters, UPS units, generators and borrowed neutrals.`,`Identify all sources from drawings and labels, isolate each, then prove dead.`,`PV faults may not blow fuses, because I_sc is close to the operating current.`],homework:{id:`F1.M2.L3.hw`,title:`Sources in a system`,brief:`A home has a grid supply, a 6 kW hybrid inverter, a 51.2 V LiFePO₄ battery and two PV strings of 9 modules (V_oc = 49 V each).`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Open-circuit voltage of one string (V)?`,answer:441,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`9 × 49 = 441 V.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`How many separate energy sources (grid, battery, PV strings counted individually, inverter output)?`,answer:5,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`Grid, battery, string 1, string 2 and the inverter output: 5.`}],written:[{prompt:`List the isolation points you would expect to find in this installation.`,model:`Grid supply main switch/breaker; inverter AC output isolator or breaker; battery DC breaker/isolator with fuse; a DC isolator for each PV string (and often an array isolator at the inverter); plus labels warning of multiple supplies.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Short-circuit current of a 12 V battery with 6 mΩ internal resistance and a 2 mΩ fault (A)?`,answer:1500,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`12/0.008 = 1500 A.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Where should a battery’s main fuse be placed?`,options:[`As close to the battery terminal as possible`,`At the load`,`In the negative after the shunt`,`Anywhere in the cable`],answer:0,explain:`It protects all cable downstream.`},{id:`q3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy in 1000 µF at 300 V (J)?`,answer:45,tol:.01,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.001 × 90 000 = 45 J.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Time to discharge 470 µF from 400 V to 50 V through 100 kΩ (s)?`,answer:97.7,tol:.02,unit:`s`,explain:`τ = 47 s; t = 47 × ln 8 = 97.7 s.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why can a PV fault persist without blowing a fuse?`,options:[`PV short-circuit current is only slightly above normal operating current`,`PV produces AC`,`PV has no voltage`,`PV fuses are always oversized`],answer:0,explain:`I_sc is close to I_mp.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Opening a switch in series with a large coil while current flows causes…`,options:[`a high-voltage spike and arcing`,`no effect`,`a reduction of voltage`,`battery charging`],answer:0,explain:`V = L·di/dt.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A grid-tied inverter must stop exporting when the grid fails because of…`,options:[`anti-islanding requirements protecting line workers`,`battery wear`,`THD limits`,`low efficiency`],answer:0,explain:`An energised island endangers people working on the grid.`},{id:`q8`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which battery precautions are correct? (select all)`,options:[`Remove metal jewellery`,`Ventilate vented lead-acid batteries`,`Disconnect the earthed negative first`,`Lay the spanner on top of the battery`],answers:[0,1,2],explain:`Never lay tools on a battery.`}]},assignment:{id:`F1.M2.asg`,title:`Module assignment: stored energy in an inverter`,brief:`A high-frequency inverter has a 48 V battery input with a 10 000 µF input capacitor, and an internal 390 V bus with 470 µF and a 180 kΩ bleeder.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy in the input capacitor at 57.6 V (J)?`,answer:16.59,tol:.02,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.01 × 57.6² = 16.6 J.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy in the HV bus at 390 V (J)?`,answer:35.74,tol:.02,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 470 × 10⁻⁶ × 152 100 = 35.7 J.`},{id:`a3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Time for the HV bus to fall to 50 V (s)?`,answer:173.8,tol:.03,unit:`s`,explain:`τ = 84.6 s; t = 84.6 × ln(7.8) = 84.6 × 2.054 = 173.8 s.`}],written:[{prompt:`Which of the two capacitors is the greater shock hazard, and why, even though the input capacitor stores a similar order of energy?`,model:`The HV bus: at 390 V it can drive a lethal current through the body, whereas 57.6 V generally cannot overcome dry skin resistance. The input capacitor is mainly a spark and burn hazard.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F1.M2.P1`,title:`Stored-energy and fault-current assessment`,description:`Calculate the hazards of the energy sources in an inverter installation. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`f1-stored-energy`,kind:`calc`,title:`Stored energy and fault current`,brief:`A 24 V battery bank (two 12 V batteries in series, each R_int = 7 mΩ) supplies an inverter with a 400 V internal bus of 330 µF and a 150 kΩ bleeder.`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Prospective short-circuit current at the bank terminals, with a 1 mΩ fault (A)?`,answer:1600,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`24/(0.014 + 0.001) = 1600 A.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy stored in the 400 V bus (J)?`,answer:26.4,tol:.02,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 330 × 10⁻⁶ × 160 000 = 26.4 J.`},{id:`c3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum waiting time for the bus to fall below 50 V (s)?`,answer:102.9,tol:.03,unit:`s`,explain:`τ = 49.5 s; t = 49.5 × ln 8 = 102.9 s.`},{id:`c4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`After waiting that time, what must you do before touching the bus?`,options:[`Measure the bus voltage with a verified tester`,`Nothing more`,`Touch it briefly to check`,`Short it with a screwdriver`],answer:0,explain:`The bleeder could have failed: prove dead.`}],explain:`Batteries deliver huge fault currents and cannot be switched off; capacitors hold energy after switch-off. Calculation tells you the scale of the hazard and how long to wait, but only measurement proves it safe.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f1-stored-energy`}}]},{id:`F1.M3`,title:`Safe isolation`,summary:`The procedure that makes electrical work safe: isolate every source, lock it off, discharge stored energy and prove dead with a verified tester. Practised here as assessed drills.`,lessons:[{id:`F1.M3.L1`,title:`Isolation and lock-out/tag-out`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Distinguish switching off, isolating and securing.`,`List the steps of a safe-isolation procedure in the correct order.`,`Explain the purpose of personal locks, tags and the restoration procedure.`,`Apply the procedure to an inverter installation with several sources.`],introduction:`Most electrical injuries to technicians happen on equipment they *thought* was dead. Safe isolation is the disciplined procedure that turns "I think it's off" into "I have proved it's dead and nobody can turn it back on". It is the same everywhere in the world in its essentials. You will practise it in this module until it is automatic.`,lecture:`### Switching off is not isolating

- **Switching off** (functional switching) stops the equipment working. A control switch may break only one conductor, may be operated by an automatic controller, and does not guarantee a safe gap.
- **[[isolation|Isolation]]** disconnects the equipment from *every* source with a device designed for isolation. Such a device gives a secure gap between its contacts and shows its position reliably.
- **Securing** prevents anyone from reconnecting the source while you work.

### The safe-isolation procedure

1. **Identify** the equipment and *every* source supplying it, from drawings, labels and inspection (lesson F1.M2.L3).
2. **Inform** the people affected that the supply will be off.
3. **Switch off** the load, so that you do not open the isolator under full current.
4. **Isolate** each source with its isolating device: main switch, breaker, DC isolator, battery fuse.
5. **Secure**: lock each isolator off with *your own* padlock and fit a warning tag with your name, the date and the reason. This is [[lockout|lock-out/tag-out]].
6. **Dissipate stored energy**: wait for the capacitors to discharge, and discharge them through a rated resistor if the procedure requires it (lesson L3).
7. **Prove dead** at the point of work with a voltage tester verified before and after (lesson L2).
8. **Work.**
9. **Restore**: make the work safe, remove tools, refit covers, inform people, then remove *your* lock and re-energise in a controlled order.

### Locks and tags

- Each person working fits their **own** lock and keeps the key. If three people are working, the isolator carries three locks (using a multi-lock hasp), and it cannot be switched on until all three have finished.
- A **tag** alone is not security: it is information. Where an isolator cannot be locked, the risk must be controlled another way, for example by removing and keeping the fuse.
- Never remove someone else's lock.

### Isolating an inverter system

For a battery-inverter-PV installation:

- open the **AC input** (grid and generator);
- open the **AC output** where you will work;
- open the **battery** fuse or breaker;
- open the **PV** DC isolators;
- lock and tag each one;
- then wait for the internal capacitors to discharge, and prove dead at the point of work. Prove the DC side as well as the AC side.`,technical:`**Why switch off the load before isolating?** Many isolators (switch-disconnectors excepted) are not designed to break full load current. Opening them on load can draw an arc, especially on DC (lesson F1.M1.L3).

**Isolation devices.** Standards define which devices are suitable for isolation: switch-disconnectors, plugs and sockets, removable fuse-carriers and some circuit breakers marked for isolation. A semiconductor switch (such as an inverter's own transistors) is **never** a means of isolation, because it can fail short-circuit or be switched on by its controller.

**Permits to work.** On larger installations a written permit-to-work system records who isolated what, who is working and when the work is complete. It is an administrative control that supports, but never replaces, locks and proving dead.`,diagrams:[{id:`isolation-flow`,caption:`The safe-isolation sequence, from identifying sources to controlled restoration.`}],formulas:[{name:`Sources to isolate`,expr:`N_isolations = number of independent sources reaching the point of work`,where:[],note:`Count grid, generator, battery, each PV string and any inverter output. Each needs its own lock.`}],examples:[{title:`Isolation plan for an inverter output breaker`,problem:`You must replace the output breaker of a hybrid inverter supplied by grid, battery and one PV string. Write the isolation plan.`,steps:[`Identify: grid (AC in), battery (DC), PV string (DC); the inverter output is generated from these`,`Inform the occupants; switch off the loads`,`Open and lock: the grid supply breaker, the battery breaker, the PV DC isolator; tag each one`,`Wait for the inverter’s capacitors to discharge (see the label)`,`Prove dead at the breaker terminals (AC) and at the inverter’s DC terminals`],answer:`Three locks (grid, battery, PV), a waiting time, and proving dead on both the AC and the DC side before touching anything.`},{title:`Two people working`,problem:`You and a colleague work on the same battery bank. How many locks go on the battery isolator, and when may it be switched on?`,steps:[`Each person fits their own lock on a multi-lock hasp`,`The isolator cannot be operated until every lock is removed by its owner`],answer:`Two locks. The isolator is switched on only when both of you have finished and removed your own locks.`}],applications:`Industrial sites, utilities and solar installers all use lock-out/tag-out. Inverter manuals list the isolation points and discharge times. The F1 practical assessments drill the sequence.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Relying on the inverter’s own on/off button for isolation.`,correction:`A control switch is not an isolator. The battery, PV and AC remain connected to the internals.`},{mistake:`Using one lock for a team.`,correction:`Each worker uses their own lock and key.`},{mistake:`Isolating the grid but forgetting the PV array.`,correction:`Identify every source from the drawings and labels; PV is live in daylight.`}],demo:{title:`Safe isolation drill`,text:`Practise the isolation sequence in this module’s practical assessment: choose the correct steps, reject the unsafe ones, and put them in order.`,link:e.lab3d(`3D laboratory: the LF-3K isolation points`,`lf3k`,`breaker`)},activities:[e.lib(`Circuit breaker (isolation)`,`circuit-breaker`),e.lab3d(`LF-3K output breaker`,`lf3k`,`breaker`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which comes first in safe isolation?`,options:[`Identify every source supplying the equipment`,`Prove dead`,`Start work`,`Remove the cover`],answer:0,explain:`You cannot isolate sources you have not identified.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why does each worker fit their own lock?`,options:[`So the supply cannot be restored until every worker has finished`,`Locks are cheap`,`To share keys`,`It is optional`],answer:0,explain:`Each person controls their own safety.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which is NOT a means of isolation?`,options:[`The inverter’s transistor switches`,`A lockable switch-disconnector`,`A removable fuse carrier`,`A lockable DC isolator`],answer:0,explain:`Semiconductors can fail short or be switched on.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`When is stored energy dealt with?`,options:[`After isolating and securing, before proving dead`,`After starting work`,`Never`,`Before identifying sources`],answer:0,explain:`Capacitors are discharged once no source can recharge them.`}],summary:[`Switching off ≠ isolating ≠ securing.`,`Procedure: identify, inform, switch off, isolate, secure, dissipate, prove dead, work, restore.`,`Every worker fits their own lock; tags inform, locks secure.`,`Semiconductor switches and control buttons are never isolation.`,`In inverter systems isolate grid, generator, battery and every PV string.`],homework:{id:`F1.M3.L1.hw`,title:`Isolation planning`,brief:`An off-grid cabin has a battery bank, two PV strings, an inverter and a petrol generator connected through a changeover switch.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`How many sources must be locked off to work on the AC distribution board (battery, each PV string, generator)?`,answer:4,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`Battery, PV string 1, PV string 2 and the generator: 4 (the inverter output is removed by isolating its sources).`}],written:[{prompt:`Write the full isolation plan, including stored energy and proving dead.`,model:`Inform users; switch off loads; stop the generator and lock its isolator/changeover off; open and lock both PV string DC isolators; open and lock the battery breaker/fuse; tag every lock; wait the inverter’s discharge time; prove dead at the AC board (L–N, L–E, N–E) with a tester verified before and after; work; restore in a controlled order.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M3.L2`,title:`Proving dead`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Explain why a voltage tester must be verified before and after testing.`,`List the conductor combinations that must be tested on AC and DC circuits.`,`Explain why non-contact detectors and multimeters on the wrong setting are not reliable proof.`,`Interpret a set of proving-dead readings and decide whether the circuit is safe.`],introduction:`Isolation can fail silently:

- the wrong breaker was opened;
- a second source exists;
- a neutral is shared;
- the isolator's contacts have welded.

**Proving dead** is the measurement that catches all of these. It is the last check between you and the conductor, and it must be done the same way every time.`,lecture:`### Test, prove, test

1. **Test the tester** on a known live source, ideally a dedicated [[proving-unit|proving unit]]. This proves the tester, its leads and its battery work.
2. **Test the circuit**: every combination of conductors at the point of work.
3. **Test the tester again** on the proving unit. This proves the tester did not fail during the test. A tester that died halfway would show "0 V" everywhere.

If the tester fails either check, the "dead" readings mean nothing.

### What to test

**Single-phase AC:**
- line–neutral (L–N);
- line–earth (L–E);
- neutral–earth (N–E).

**DC (battery, PV):**
- positive–negative;
- positive–earth;
- negative–earth.

**Three-phase:** every phase to every other phase, to neutral and to earth.

Testing only L–N can miss a dangerous voltage. For example, a [[borrowed-neutral|borrowed neutral]] can sit at tens of volts above earth while L–N reads zero.

### Which instrument?

- **A two-pole voltage tester** designed for proving dead, used with a proving unit. It has fused, shrouded probes, an appropriate [[measurement-category|measurement category]] rating, and it indicates without needing a range selection.
- **A multimeter** can be used if it is suitably rated and on the correct range, but a wrong setting (for example, current mode) can create a short circuit or show nothing. Many organisations therefore require a dedicated tester.
- **Non-contact voltage detectors** ("volt sticks") are useful for a quick check, but they are **not proof of dead**. They can miss DC entirely, miss shielded cables, and fail silently.

### After proving dead

If the circuit is dead, work may start, with the isolation still locked. If *any* reading shows a voltage, the circuit is **not** dead:

- stop, and do not touch anything;
- find the unisolated source;
- isolate it;
- test again.`,technical:`**Why proving units?** A proving unit generates a known test voltage on demand, so a tester can be checked anywhere, including where no known live source is nearby. Using a live socket as the "known source" works, but it exposes the tester user to mains.

**Phantom (ghost) voltages.** High-impedance multimeters can read tens of volts on an isolated conductor that runs alongside live cables, through capacitive coupling. Testers designed for proving dead apply a small load that collapses such phantom readings. A reading that collapses under load suggests coupling rather than a real source, but the rule remains: **any unexplained voltage means not dead** until it is understood.

**DC capacitors.** On the DC side of an inverter, a reading that falls steadily while you watch is a capacitor still discharging. Wait, and test again.`,diagrams:[{id:`isolation-flow`,caption:`Proving dead sits between securing the isolation and starting work: test the tester, test every combination, test the tester again.`}],formulas:[{name:`Combinations to test (single-phase AC)`,expr:`L–N, L–E, N–E  (DC: +/−, + to earth, − to earth)`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Interpreting readings`,problem:`After isolating a socket circuit you measure: tester on proving unit OK; L–N 0 V; L–E 0 V; N–E 48 V; tester on proving unit OK. Is the circuit dead?`,steps:[`The tester is proven before and after, so the readings are valid`,`N–E = 48 V means the neutral is at a voltage relative to earth`,`Likely cause: the neutral is shared with another live circuit (a borrowed neutral)`],answer:`Not dead. Stop and find the other circuit sharing the neutral, isolate it, and test again.`},{title:`A tester that failed`,problem:`A technician tests the tester on the proving unit (OK), measures L–N, L–E, N–E (all 0 V), but forgets the final check. Later the tester is found to have a broken lead. What could have happened?`,steps:[`If the lead broke before the measurements, every reading would show 0 V regardless of the actual voltage`,`Only the final proving check would have revealed the failure`],answer:`The circuit could have been live while reading “dead”. The final proving check is essential.`}],applications:`Proving dead is required before work on any conductor in electrical safety regulations worldwide. The F1 prove-dead drill reproduces it with a virtual tester, including a scenario in which isolation was incomplete.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using a non-contact detector as proof of dead.`,correction:`It is a screening tool only. Prove dead with a two-pole tester verified on a proving unit.`},{mistake:`Testing only line to neutral.`,correction:`Test all combinations including to earth; a borrowed neutral or earth fault may otherwise be missed.`},{mistake:`Skipping the second proving check.`,correction:`A tester can fail during use; re-prove it after testing.`}],demo:{title:`Prove-dead drill`,text:`In the practical assessment for this module, use a virtual tester on an isolated circuit, record every reading, and decide whether it is safe to work.`,link:e.proc(`Fuse check: a procedure that begins with isolation`,`fuse`,`fuse-check`)},activities:[e.lib(`Protective earth`,`protective-earth`),e.cmp(`Voltage versus current sensing`,`voltage-vs-current-sensing`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is the tester checked on the proving unit after testing?`,options:[`To prove it did not fail during the test`,`To recharge it`,`To calibrate the circuit`,`It is not necessary`],answer:0,explain:`A tester failing mid-test would show false “dead” readings.`},{id:`k2`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which combinations are tested on a single-phase AC circuit? (select all)`,options:[`L–N`,`L–E`,`N–E`,`L–L`],answers:[0,1,2],explain:`Single-phase has one line conductor.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A non-contact detector shows nothing on an isolated DC battery cable. You conclude…`,options:[`Nothing: these detectors cannot detect DC; prove dead with a two-pole tester`,`The cable is dead`,`The battery is flat`,`The detector is faulty`],answer:0,explain:`Most non-contact detectors respond only to AC fields.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`L–N reads 0 V but L–E reads 230 V. The circuit is…`,options:[`live: stop and find the source`,`dead`,`safe because L–N is zero`,`only dangerous to the neutral`],answer:0,explain:`Any voltage between conductors and earth means not dead.`}],summary:[`Test the tester on a proving unit, test every combination, test the tester again.`,`Single-phase: L–N, L–E, N–E; DC: +/−, + to earth, − to earth.`,`Use a two-pole tester; non-contact detectors are not proof.`,`Any reading above zero means not dead: stop, find the source, isolate, retest.`,`Falling DC readings indicate capacitors still discharging.`],homework:{id:`F1.M3.L2.hw`,title:`Reading the readings`,brief:`Decide whether each set of proving-dead readings shows a dead circuit.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Tester OK before; L–N 0 V, L–E 0 V, N–E 0 V; tester OK after.`,options:[`Dead: work may start (with isolation locked)`,`Not dead`],answer:0,explain:`All combinations tested with a verified tester.`},{id:`h2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Tester OK before; + to − 38 V and falling; tester OK after.`,options:[`Not yet dead: capacitors still discharging, wait and retest`,`Dead`],answer:0,explain:`A falling DC voltage is stored charge.`},{id:`h3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Tester not checked before; all readings 0 V; tester OK after.`,options:[`Not valid: the tester was not proven before testing`,`Dead`],answer:0,explain:`Both checks are required.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why proving dead is still necessary after you personally opened and locked the isolator.`,model:`The isolator may be the wrong one, its contacts may have failed, a second source or back-feed may exist (inverter, PV, borrowed neutral), or stored energy may remain. Only a measurement at the point of work confirms the conductors are actually dead.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M3.L3`,title:`Capacitor discharge and verification`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`State the safe method for discharging a capacitor bank and verifying it.`,`Choose a discharge resistor for a given capacitor bank and time.`,`Calculate the peak discharge current and energy dissipated.`,`Explain why discharge is verified by measurement after it is done.`],introduction:`Inverters and solar equipment contain capacitor banks that can stay charged after isolation (lesson F1.M2.L2). Before touching any internal part, the stored energy must be removed safely and the result proved by measurement. This lesson gives the method. The 3D laboratory's guided procedure, which opens once F1 is passed, lets you carry it out on the LF-3K.`,lecture:`### Wait first

Most equipment has [[bleeder]] resistors that discharge the capacitors after isolation. The manufacturer states the discharge time on a label or in the manual, often several minutes. **Wait at least that long.**

### Then measure

Measure the voltage across the capacitor bank with a voltage tester verified on a proving unit, exactly as in proving dead. If the voltage is below the safe level given in the procedure (this course uses 50 V), discharge is complete. Re-check large banks after a minute or two for recovery voltage.

### If discharge is needed

If the voltage is still above the safe level (a failed bleeder, or a procedure that requires active discharge):

1. Use a **discharge tool**: a resistor of suitable resistance, power and voltage rating, with insulated, shrouded probes.
2. Connect it across the capacitor terminals, **never** a screwdriver or a wire. A dead short releases the energy in microseconds as an arc, can weld the tool, injure you and damage the capacitor.
3. Hold it for at least 5 time constants (τ = R·C).
4. Remove it and **measure again**.

### Choosing the resistor

- **Resistance:** high enough to keep the initial current modest (I_peak = V₀/R), low enough to discharge in a reasonable time (t = RC·ln(V₀/V_s)).
- **Energy:** all of the stored energy ½CV² ends up in the resistor, within seconds. A resistor rated for a few watts continuously can absorb this as a short pulse, but its pulse rating must be adequate. That is the reason for using purpose-made tools.
- **Voltage rating:** at least the bank's maximum voltage.

### Verification is the proof

Calculations predict; measurements prove. A discharge resistor with a broken lead does nothing, and the meter is the only thing that will tell you so.`,technical:`**Pulse energy versus continuous power.** A 10 W resistor can absorb far more than 10 J if the energy arrives in a short pulse, because its thermal mass heats up gradually. Manufacturers give pulse-energy curves. The 3D laboratory's discharge simulator uses a simplified rule (rated W × 5 s) to illustrate this. Real tools are specified by their makers.

**Recovery voltage (dielectric absorption).** After a large electrolytic capacitor is discharged and the resistor removed, its voltage can creep back up by a few per cent of the original value over minutes. For high-voltage banks, procedures may require shorting links to be fitted after discharge.

**Never discharge through the body or a meter.** A meter on its voltage range has a very high resistance and will hardly discharge anything. On a current or resistance range it can short the capacitor and be damaged.`,diagrams:[{id:`rc-curve`,caption:`Discharge through a rated resistor: the voltage falls by 63 % per time constant.`}],formulas:[{name:`Peak discharge current`,expr:`I_peak = V₀ / R`,where:[[`V₀`,`initial capacitor voltage`,`V`],[`R`,`discharge resistance`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Discharge time`,expr:`t = R · C · ln(V₀ / V_s)`,where:[[`V_s`,`safe voltage`,`V`]]},{name:`Energy absorbed by the resistor`,expr:`E = ½ · C · (V₀² − V_s²)`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Choosing a discharge resistor`,problem:`A 470 µF bank at 400 V must be discharged to below 50 V within 10 s. What is the largest resistance that achieves this, and what is the peak current?`,steps:[`t = RC·ln(V₀/V_s) → R = t / (C·ln(V₀/V_s)) = 10 / (470 × 10⁻⁶ × ln 8) = 10 / (470 × 10⁻⁶ × 2.079) = 10 234 Ω`,`Choose 10 kΩ (a little lower); I_peak = 400 / 10 000 = 0.04 A`],answer:`About 10 kΩ, giving a modest 40 mA peak. The resistor must be rated above 400 V and absorb about 37 J as a pulse.`,check:`E = ½ × 470 × 10⁻⁶ × (400² − 50²) = 37.0 J. ✓`},{title:`Why not a screwdriver?`,problem:`The same bank is shorted by a screwdriver with 0.05 Ω total resistance. Estimate the peak current.`,steps:[`Peak current: I_peak = V₀ / R`,`I_peak = 400 / 0.05 = 8000 A (limited in practice by capacitor ESR and inductance, but still kiloamperes)`],answer:`Thousands of amperes for microseconds: a violent arc that can weld the tool, throw molten metal and damage the capacitor.`}],applications:`Service procedures for inverters, drives and power supplies all include a discharge-and-verify step. The 3D laboratory refuses unsafe discharge methods (such as a screwdriver short) and explains why.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Shorting capacitors with a screwdriver.`,correction:`Use a rated discharge resistor with insulated probes.`},{mistake:`Assuming the discharge worked because the resistor was applied.`,correction:`Always measure afterwards; leads break and contacts fail.`},{mistake:`Using a meter on its resistance range to “drain” a capacitor.`,correction:`Meters are not discharge devices and can be damaged.`}],demo:{title:`Discharge timer`,text:`Use the capacitor discharge demonstration to choose a resistor that brings a 400 V bank below 50 V in a set time, and read the peak current and the absorbed energy.`,link:e.lab3d(`3D laboratory: guided discharge (after F1)`,`lf3k`,`c5`),embed:`cap-discharge`},activities:[e.proc(`Capacitor ESR and discharge check`,`capacitor`,`cap-esr`),e.lib(`Capacitor`,`capacitor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Peak current discharging 300 V through 15 kΩ (mA)?`,answer:20,tol:.01,unit:`mA`,explain:`300/15 000 = 0.02 A.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy absorbed discharging 1000 µF from 100 V to 0 V (J)?`,answer:5,tol:.01,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.001 × 100² = 5 J.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`After applying a discharge resistor, you must…`,options:[`measure the voltage with a verified tester`,`touch the terminals to check`,`assume it is discharged`,`apply it again without measuring`],answer:0,explain:`Measurement is the proof.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is a very low-resistance short dangerous for discharging?`,options:[`It releases the energy in microseconds as an arc with kiloampere currents`,`It is too slow`,`It charges the capacitor`,`It is safe`],answer:0,explain:`I = V/R becomes enormous.`}],summary:[`Wait the stated discharge time, then measure with a verified tester.`,`If discharge is needed, use a rated resistor with insulated probes for ≥ 5τ.`,`R from t = RC·ln(V₀/V_s); peak current V₀/R; the resistor absorbs ½C(V₀² − V_s²).`,`Never use a screwdriver, a wire or a meter to discharge.`,`Always verify by measurement; re-check large banks for recovery voltage.`],homework:{id:`F1.M3.L3.hw`,title:`Discharge design`,brief:`A 680 µF bank at 380 V must reach 50 V within 15 s.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Largest discharge resistance (kΩ)?`,answer:10.88,tol:.03,unit:`kΩ`,explain:`R = 15 / (680 × 10⁻⁶ × ln(7.6)) = 15 / (680 × 10⁻⁶ × 2.028) = 10.88 kΩ.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Peak current with a 10 kΩ resistor (mA)?`,answer:38,tol:.01,unit:`mA`,explain:`380/10 000 = 38 mA.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy absorbed from 380 V to 50 V (J)?`,answer:48.25,tol:.02,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 680 × 10⁻⁶ × (144 400 − 2500) = 48.2 J.`}],written:[{prompt:`Describe the complete discharge-and-verify procedure for this bank, starting from an isolated inverter.`,model:`Confirm isolation is locked; wait the labelled discharge time; verify the tester on a proving unit; measure the bank voltage; if above 50 V apply a rated 10 kΩ discharge tool across the terminals for at least 5τ (≈ 34 s); remove it; measure again (and again after a minute for recovery); re-verify the tester; only then touch internal parts.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What does “securing” an isolation mean?`,options:[`Locking and tagging the isolator so it cannot be switched on`,`Switching off the load`,`Testing the tester`,`Removing the cover`],answer:0,explain:`Securing prevents re-energisation.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which order is correct?`,options:[`Isolate → secure → dissipate stored energy → prove dead`,`Prove dead → isolate → secure`,`Work → isolate → prove dead`,`Dissipate → isolate → work`],answer:0,explain:`Prove dead only after sources are secured and stored energy removed.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A reading of 40 V between neutral and earth on an isolated circuit suggests…`,options:[`a shared (borrowed) neutral from another live circuit`,`a safe circuit`,`a faulty earth rod only`,`a flat tester battery`],answer:0,explain:`The neutral carries another circuit’s current.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which instrument is acceptable proof of dead?`,options:[`A two-pole voltage tester verified before and after on a proving unit`,`A non-contact detector`,`A multimeter on the current range`,`The equipment’s display`],answer:0,explain:`Only a verified two-pole tester proves dead.`},{id:`q5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Largest R to discharge 1000 µF from 200 V to 50 V within 5 s (kΩ)?`,answer:3.607,tol:.03,unit:`kΩ`,explain:`R = 5/(0.001 × ln 4) = 5/1.386 = 3.61 kΩ.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Three technicians work on one battery bank. The isolator carries…`,options:[`three personal locks`,`one shared lock`,`a tag only`,`no lock if they are careful`],answer:0,explain:`Each worker has their own lock.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A DC bus reads 62 V and falling during proving dead. You…`,options:[`wait and test again until below the safe level`,`start work`,`conclude the tester is faulty`,`short it with a screwdriver`],answer:0,explain:`The capacitors are still discharging.`},{id:`q8`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which are NOT valid means of isolation? (select all)`,options:[`The inverter’s on/off button`,`The inverter’s transistors`,`A lockable switch-disconnector`,`A removed and retained fuse`],answers:[0,1],explain:`Control buttons and semiconductors are never isolation.`}]},assignment:{id:`F1.M3.asg`,title:`Module assignment: isolation procedure for an inverter repair`,brief:`A 5 kW hybrid inverter (grid input, 48 V battery, one PV string) has a 420 V internal bus with 560 µF and 200 kΩ bleeders. Its output terminal block must be replaced.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Bus energy at 420 V (J)?`,answer:49.39,tol:.02,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 560 × 10⁻⁶ × 176 400 = 49.4 J.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Bleeder time to 50 V (s)?`,answer:238.3,tol:.03,unit:`s`,explain:`τ = 112 s; t = 112 × ln(8.4) = 112 × 2.128 = 238 s.`},{id:`a3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Number of personal locks needed if two technicians isolate grid, battery and PV?`,answer:6,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`3 isolation points × 2 workers = 6 locks (on multi-lock hasps).`}],written:[{prompt:`Write the complete procedure from identification to restoration.`,model:`Identify grid, battery and PV sources from the labels and diagram; inform occupants; switch off loads; open and lock (each technician’s lock) the grid breaker, battery breaker and PV isolator, tag each; wait ≥ 4 minutes; verify the tester on a proving unit; prove dead at the output terminals (L–N, L–E, N–E) and on the DC bus (+/−, + and − to earth); re-verify the tester; replace the terminal block; refit covers, remove tools, inform; each technician removes their own locks; energise in the manufacturer’s order and check operation.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F1.M3.P1`,title:`Safe isolation sequence drill`,description:`Build the safe-isolation procedure for a solar-battery-inverter system: choose the correct steps, reject the unsafe ones and put them in order.`,task:{id:`f1-isolation-sequence`,kind:`sequence`,title:`Isolating a solar-battery-inverter system`,brief:`You must work on the AC output wiring of an off-grid inverter supplied by a battery bank and a PV string. Choose the steps you would carry out, in order. Some offered steps are wrong or unsafe.`,steps:[`Identify every source from the drawings and labels: battery, PV string, inverter output`,`Inform the people who use the system`,`Switch off the loads and the inverter`,`Open the PV DC isolator and the battery breaker`,`Lock and tag each isolator with your own lock`,`Wait for the inverter’s capacitors to discharge (labelled time)`,`Verify the voltage tester on the proving unit`,`Test L–N, L–E and N–E at the point of work, and the DC terminals`,`Verify the voltage tester on the proving unit again`],distractors:[{text:`Cover the PV array with a sheet instead of opening the isolator`,why:`A covering can slip or leak light; the PV string must be isolated with its DC isolator.`},{text:`Check for voltage with a non-contact detector only`,why:`Non-contact detectors are not proof of dead and usually cannot detect DC.`},{text:`Discharge the capacitors by shorting them with a screwdriver`,why:`A short releases the energy as a violent arc; use a rated discharge resistor if needed, then measure.`}],explain:`The sequence is identify → inform → switch off → isolate → secure → dissipate stored energy → test the tester → test → test the tester. Every source must be isolated and the result proved by measurement.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f1-isolation-sequence`}},{id:`F1.M3.P2`,title:`Prove-dead drill with a virtual tester`,description:`Perform proving dead on an isolated socket circuit, read each result and decide whether it is safe to work.`,task:{id:`f1-prove-dead`,kind:`procedure`,title:`Proving dead an isolated circuit`,brief:`The breaker for a workshop socket circuit has been opened, locked and tagged. Carry out proving dead at the socket. Choose actions one at a time; each result is shown. Then record your conclusion.`,actions:[{id:`prove1`,label:`Check the voltage tester on the proving unit`,result:`Tester indicates 230 V on the proving unit: working.`},{id:`ln`,label:`Test line to neutral at the socket`,result:`L–N: 0 V`},{id:`le`,label:`Test line to earth at the socket`,result:`L–E: 0 V`},{id:`ne`,label:`Test neutral to earth at the socket`,result:`N–E: 46 V`},{id:`prove2`,label:`Check the voltage tester on the proving unit again`,result:`Tester indicates 230 V on the proving unit: still working.`},{id:`ncv`,label:`Hold a non-contact detector near the socket`,result:`The detector does not light. (Not proof of dead.)`},{id:`touch`,label:`Touch the socket terminals to confirm they are dead`,result:`—`,unsafe:`Never touch conductors to “test” them. Use a verified tester.`},{id:`trust`,label:`Start work because the breaker is locked off`,result:`—`,unsafe:`A locked breaker is not proof: prove dead at the point of work first.`}],required:[`prove1`,`ln`,`le`,`ne`,`prove2`],conclusion:{prompt:`What do you conclude?`,options:[`The circuit is dead: start work`,`The circuit is NOT dead: the neutral is at 46 V, probably shared with another live circuit; stop, find and isolate it, then test again`,`The tester is faulty`],answer:1,explain:`A verified tester showed 46 V between neutral and earth. The neutral is shared with another circuit that is still live (a borrowed neutral), so the circuit is not safe.`},explain:`Test the tester, test every combination, test the tester again — and believe the readings. Testing only L–N would have missed the live neutral.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f1-prove-dead`}}]},{id:`F1.M4`,title:`Protection, earthing and workshop practice`,summary:`How fuses, breakers, earthing, RCDs and insulation protect people and property, and how to organise safe practical work: PPE, rated tools and instruments, risk assessment and knowing when to stop.`,lessons:[{id:`F1.M4.L1`,title:`Fuses and circuit breakers`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Explain what overcurrent protection protects, and what it does not.`,`Apply the coordination rule I_b ≤ I_n ≤ I_z to choose a protective device.`,`Distinguish overload and short-circuit protection, and explain breaking capacity.`,`Select a fuse for a battery circuit from its maximum current and cable rating.`],introduction:`A [[fuse]] or a [[breaker|circuit breaker]] is the device that opens a circuit when the current becomes dangerous. Its job is very specific: it protects the **cable and equipment from overheating and fire**. It does not protect people from shock, and it does not protect sensitive semiconductors, which fail far faster than any fuse can act. Choosing it correctly is a basic engineering skill.`,lecture:`### Two kinds of overcurrent

- **Overload:** a moderate excess current that persists, such as too many appliances on a circuit or a motor straining. The cable heats slowly, so protection can take seconds to minutes.
- **Short circuit:** a fault with almost no resistance, drawing enormous current. Protection must act within milliseconds, before the cable is damaged.

### How the devices work

- **A fuse** contains a calibrated element that melts when the current is too high for too long. It is simple, fast and reliable, but must be replaced after operating.
- **A circuit breaker** combines two mechanisms:
  - a *thermal* trip (a bimetal strip) for overloads;
  - a *magnetic* trip (a coil) for short circuits.
  It can be reset, and some types are also suitable for isolation.

Both have a **time–current characteristic**: the larger the overcurrent, the faster they operate.

### The coordination rule

For a circuit with design current I_b, protective device rating I_n and cable current-carrying capacity [[ampacity|I_z]]:

**I_b ≤ I_n ≤ I_z**

- The device must carry the normal current without operating (I_n ≥ I_b).
- It must operate before the cable overheats (I_n ≤ I_z).

Battery and inverter circuits commonly also apply a margin, I_n ≥ 1.25 × the maximum continuous current, so that the device does not age or trip in normal use. This is the rule used in course S1.

### Breaking capacity

A protective device must be able to **interrupt the largest fault current** that can flow at its location (the prospective short-circuit current) without exploding or welding shut. A battery bank can deliver several kiloamperes (lesson F1.M2.L1), so battery fuses must have a breaking capacity above that, at the DC voltage. A cheap automotive fuse may not interrupt a large battery's fault current safely.

### What protection does *not* do

- It does **not prevent shock**. A few tens of milliamperes through a person is far below any fuse rating. Earthing and RCDs handle that (lesson L2).
- It does **not protect semiconductors**. A MOSFET fails within microseconds of a short. Inverters need electronic current limiting.
- It does **not** stop a PV fault whose current is close to normal (lesson F1.M2.L3).`,technical:`**I²t.** A fuse's let-through energy is characterised by I²t, the integral of current squared over its operating time. A cable can withstand a maximum I²t = k²S², where S is its cross-section and k a constant for its conductor and insulation (about 115 for PVC-insulated copper in installation standards). The fuse's I²t must be below the cable's.

**DC ratings.** A fuse or breaker rated "250 V AC" may be rated much lower for DC, or not at all, because a DC arc does not self-extinguish (lesson F1.M1.L3). Always check the DC voltage rating, and for breakers check polarity marking: some DC breakers must be connected the right way round.

**Selectivity.** In larger systems, devices are chosen so that the one closest to a fault operates first, leaving the rest of the installation working. That is called discrimination or selectivity.`,diagrams:[{id:`protection-chain`,caption:`Each protective device protects against a specific harm: fuses and breakers protect wiring.`}],formulas:[{name:`Coordination rule`,expr:`I_b ≤ I_n ≤ I_z`,where:[[`I_b`,`design (normal maximum) current`,`A`],[`I_n`,`device rating`,`A`],[`I_z`,`cable current-carrying capacity`,`A`]]},{name:`Margin used for battery and inverter circuits`,expr:`I_n ≥ 1.25 · I_max  (next standard size)`,where:[[`I_max`,`maximum continuous current`,`A`]]},{name:`Breaking capacity requirement`,expr:`I_cu ≥ I_sc (at the device, at the system voltage)`,where:[[`I_cu`,`rated breaking capacity`,`A`],[`I_sc`,`prospective short-circuit current`,`A`]]}],examples:[{title:`Fuse for an inverter battery cable`,problem:`A 1000 W, 12 V inverter draws at most 105 A (at low battery). The cable is rated I_z = 160 A. Standard fuse sizes include 100, 125, 150 and 200 A. Choose the fuse.`,steps:[`Margin: I_n ≥ 1.25 × 105 = 131 A → next standard size 150 A`,`Check I_n ≤ I_z: 150 ≤ 160 ✓`],answer:`A 150 A fuse, with a DC voltage rating ≥ 14.4 V and a breaking capacity above the battery’s fault current.`},{title:`A cable that is too small`,problem:`The same inverter is wired with cable rated I_z = 120 A. Can the 150 A fuse protect it?`,steps:[`I_n = 150 A > I_z = 120 A ✗ (the cable could overheat without the fuse operating)`,`Options: larger cable (I_z ≥ 150 A), or reduce the load so a smaller fuse is adequate`],answer:`No. The fuse must never be larger than the cable rating. Use a larger cable.`}],applications:`Every circuit in a building or an inverter system has overcurrent protection sized by these rules. Course S1's configurator calculates fuse and breaker sizes in exactly this way. Course S2 applies them to PV strings and battery banks.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Replacing a blown fuse with a larger one “because it kept blowing”.`,correction:`A blowing fuse indicates a fault or an overload. A larger fuse removes the cable’s protection: find the cause.`},{mistake:`Using an AC-only breaker on a battery circuit.`,correction:`Use devices rated for DC at the system voltage, with adequate breaking capacity.`},{mistake:`Believing a fuse protects people from shock.`,correction:`Shock currents are far below fuse ratings; earthing and RCDs protect people.`}],demo:{title:`Fuse versus breaker`,text:`Read the library’s comparison of fuses and circuit breakers: operating principles, characteristics, DC ratings and when each is used.`,link:e.cmp(`Fuse versus circuit breaker`,`fuse-vs-breaker`)},activities:[e.lib(`Fuse`,`fuse`),e.lib(`Circuit breaker`,`circuit-breaker`),e.proc(`Fuse check procedure`,`fuse`,`fuse-check`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum fuse rating for a 60 A maximum current with the 1.25 margin (A)?`,answer:75,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 60 = 75 A, then round up to a standard size.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A cable rated 100 A is protected by a 125 A fuse. This is…`,options:[`wrong: I_n must not exceed I_z`,`correct`,`correct if the fuse is fast`,`only wrong for AC`],answer:0,explain:`I_n ≤ I_z.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What does breaking capacity describe?`,options:[`The largest fault current the device can safely interrupt`,`The normal current rating`,`The voltage drop`,`The number of operations`],answer:0,explain:`It must exceed the prospective fault current.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Overcurrent devices primarily protect…`,options:[`cables and equipment from overheating and fire`,`people from shock`,`MOSFETs from short circuits`,`batteries from sulphation`],answer:0,explain:`That is their purpose.`}],summary:[`Fuses and breakers protect wiring and equipment from overload and short circuit.`,`Coordination: I_b ≤ I_n ≤ I_z; in battery circuits I_n ≥ 1.25·I_max.`,`Breaking capacity must exceed the prospective fault current at the system (DC) voltage.`,`They do not protect against shock or protect semiconductors.`,`Never up-rate a blowing fuse: find the fault.`],homework:{id:`F1.M4.L1.hw`,title:`Protection selection`,brief:`Standard sizes: 20, 25, 32, 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125 A.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A 24 V inverter draws 38 A maximum. Standard fuse with the 1.25 margin (A)?`,answer:50,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 38 = 47.5 → 50 A.`},{id:`h2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The cable is rated 45 A. Is the 50 A fuse acceptable?`,options:[`No: use a larger cable`,`Yes`],answer:0,explain:`I_n (50) > I_z (45).`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery fault current if V = 25.6 V and R_int + wiring = 8 mΩ (A)?`,answer:3200,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`25.6/0.008 = 3200 A: the fuse breaking capacity must exceed this.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why a fuse that keeps blowing must not be replaced with a larger one.`,model:`The fuse is sized to protect the cable; a larger fuse would let the cable overheat before operating, risking fire. Repeated blowing indicates an overload or fault that must be found and corrected.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M4.L2`,title:`Earthing, bonding, RCDs and insulation`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Explain how protective earthing and automatic disconnection prevent dangerous touch voltages.`,`Explain the operating principle of an RCD and its limits.`,`Distinguish basic insulation, double insulation and the IP code.`,`Calculate a fault current and touch voltage in a simple earth-fault loop.`],introduction:`Fuses protect cables; **earthing and RCDs protect people**. If insulation fails inside an appliance and the line conductor touches its metal case, the case becomes live. Earthing and protective devices are designed so that this fault is cleared within a fraction of a second, before anyone can be harmed. Insulation is the first barrier; earthing and RCDs are the safety net.`,lecture:`### Protective earthing and automatic disconnection

Exposed metal parts (cases, frames, enclosures) are connected by a [[protective-earth]] conductor (PE, green/yellow) to the installation's earthing system. If a line conductor touches the case:

1. A large **fault current** flows from line, through the case and the PE conductor, back to the source.
2. This current is large enough to make the fuse or breaker operate quickly. That is **automatic disconnection of supply**.
3. While the fault persists, the case rises to a [[touch-voltage|touch voltage]]. The design keeps this low or short-lived.

The path the fault current takes is the **earth fault loop**. Its impedance must be low enough to give a fault current that operates the protection within the required time.

### Bonding

**Equipotential bonding** connects metal parts that a person could touch at the same time (pipes, structural steel, equipment frames). They therefore rise to the same voltage during a fault, so there is no dangerous voltage *between* them.

### The RCD

A residual current device ([[rcd]]) passes the line and neutral conductors through a sensing core.

- Normally the outgoing and returning currents are equal and cancel.
- If some current leaks to earth (through a damaged cable, a wet appliance, or a person), the imbalance is detected. A 30 mA RCD disconnects at a residual current of 30 mA or less, typically within tens of milliseconds for larger leakage.

**Limits:** an RCD does not detect line-to-neutral contact (the current returns on the neutral), nor overcurrent. It is *additional* protection.

### Insulation

- **Basic insulation** covers live parts so they cannot be touched.
- **[[double-insulation|Double or reinforced insulation (Class II)]]** adds a second independent layer, so that a single insulation failure cannot make accessible parts live. Class II equipment has no earth connection.
- **Enclosures and the [[ip-code|IP code]]:** IP2X means fingers cannot reach live parts; IPX4 is splash-proof; IP65 is dust-tight and protected against water jets. Outdoor PV and inverter equipment needs a suitable IP rating.

### Earthing in inverter systems

- Inverter chassis are bonded to PE.
- The AC output's neutral–earth arrangement depends on the installation's earthing system and the manufacturer's instructions. In off-grid systems the inverter often creates the neutral–earth bond itself.
- PV array frames are bonded.

These are installation design decisions covered in course S3. The F1 principle is simple: **exposed metal must be earthed or double-insulated, and faults must disconnect quickly.**`,technical:`**Fault current calculation.** In a simplified TN system, the fault current is I_f = U₀ / Z_s, where U₀ is the line-to-earth voltage (230 V) and Z_s the earth-fault loop impedance. The protective device must operate within the required disconnection time at I_f. For example, a type B breaker of rating I_n operates magnetically at 3–5 × I_n.

**Touch voltage.** During a fault, the case voltage is roughly I_f × R_PE (the resistance of the protective conductor from the case to the main earthing point). Equipotential bonding reduces the voltage *between* simultaneously accessible parts.

**Earthing systems.** TN-S, TN-C-S and TT differ in how the neutral and earth are connected and where. In TT systems, earth-fault currents are small (limited by the earth-electrode resistance), so RCDs provide the disconnection. That is specialist installation design, governed by local regulations (course S3).`,diagrams:[{id:`fault-loop`,caption:`An earth fault: current flows from line, through the faulty case and the protective earth, back to the source, operating the protection.`},{id:`rcd`,caption:`RCD principle: line and neutral currents cancel in the core; a leak to earth leaves a residual current that trips the device.`}],formulas:[{name:`Earth-fault current`,expr:`I_f = U₀ / Z_s`,where:[[`U₀`,`line-to-earth voltage`,`V`],[`Z_s`,`earth-fault loop impedance`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Touch voltage on a faulty case`,expr:`U_t ≈ I_f · R_PE`,where:[[`R_PE`,`protective conductor resistance from the case`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Residual current`,expr:`I_Δ = I_line − I_neutral`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Will the breaker trip?`,problem:`A 230 V circuit has an earth-fault loop impedance of 1.15 Ω and is protected by a 16 A type B breaker, which trips magnetically at 5 × I_n. Will an earth fault trip it instantly?`,steps:[`I_f = 230 / 1.15 = 200 A`,`Magnetic trip at 5 × 16 = 80 A`,`200 A > 80 A ✓`],answer:`Yes. 200 A operates the magnetic trip within a fraction of a second.`},{title:`Residual current`,problem:`A washing machine draws 8.000 A on the line conductor and 7.975 A returns on the neutral. What is the residual current, and will a 30 mA RCD trip?`,steps:[`I_Δ = 8.000 − 7.975 = 0.025 A = 25 mA`,`25 mA is below 30 mA; the RCD may hold (it trips somewhere between 50 % and 100 % of 30 mA)`],answer:`25 mA of leakage. The RCD may or may not trip; such a leak shows insulation is deteriorating and needs investigating.`}],applications:`Earthing and RCDs are required in building wiring regulations worldwide. Inverter installation manuals specify the earthing arrangement. Course S3 teaches the testing used during commissioning: continuity, insulation resistance, earth-fault loop impedance and RCD trip time.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Removing the earth wire because the RCD keeps tripping.`,correction:`The RCD is detecting a leak. Find the fault; removing the earth makes the equipment dangerous.`},{mistake:`Assuming double-insulated equipment needs an earth.`,correction:`Class II equipment is designed to be safe without an earth connection; it must not be earthed through its accessible parts.`},{mistake:`Believing an RCD protects against touching line and neutral together.`,correction:`No current leaks to earth in that case, so the RCD cannot detect it.`}],demo:{title:`Earth connections in a real inverter`,text:`Find the protective earth stud and the bonding of the enclosure in the LF-3K model, and read the library entry on protective earth.`,link:e.lab3d(`LF-3K protective earth`,`lf3k`,`pe`)},activities:[e.lib(`Protective earth`,`protective-earth`),e.proc(`PE continuity test`,`protective-earth`,`pe-continuity`),e.lab3d(`LF-3K cover (bonded enclosure)`,`lf3k`,`cover`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Earth-fault current with U₀ = 230 V and Z_s = 0.92 Ω (A)?`,answer:250,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`230/0.92 = 250 A.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Residual current: line 5.040 A, neutral 5.000 A (mA)?`,answer:40,tol:.01,unit:`mA`,explain:`0.040 A = 40 mA: a 30 mA RCD trips.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What makes the fuse or breaker operate on an earth fault?`,options:[`A large fault current through the protective earth conductor`,`The RCD only`,`The touch voltage`,`The insulation`],answer:0,explain:`Low earth-fault loop impedance gives a large current.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`IP2X means…`,options:[`fingers cannot reach live parts`,`fully waterproof`,`dust-tight`,`no protection`],answer:0,explain:`First digit 2: protected against objects ≥ 12.5 mm (fingers).`}],summary:[`Earthing plus automatic disconnection clears faults on exposed metal quickly.`,`Bonding keeps simultaneously accessible metal parts at the same voltage.`,`RCDs trip on residual (leakage) current, e.g. 30 mA; they do not detect line–neutral contact.`,`Insulation (basic, double), enclosures and IP ratings are the first barrier.`,`Never remove earths to stop RCD trips: find the leak.`],homework:{id:`F1.M4.L2.hw`,title:`Earth-fault calculations`,brief:`A 230 V circuit protected by a 20 A type B breaker (magnetic trip 5 × I_n).`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum Z_s for the magnetic trip to operate (Ω)?`,answer:2.3,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`I_f ≥ 100 A → Z_s ≤ 230/100 = 2.3 Ω.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fault current if Z_s = 0.8 Ω (A)?`,answer:287.5,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`230/0.8 = 287.5 A.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Touch voltage if R_PE = 0.3 Ω carries that current (V)?`,answer:86.25,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`287.5 × 0.3 = 86 V, which exists only until the breaker trips (milliseconds).`}],written:[{prompt:`Why is an RCD added even when earthing and breakers are correctly designed?`,model:`Breakers need a large fault current; an RCD detects small leakage currents (such as through a person or damp insulation) that would never operate a breaker, and disconnects quickly, giving additional protection against shock.`}]}}},{id:`F1.M4.L3`,title:`Workshop practice: PPE, tools, measurement categories and risk assessment`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Apply the hierarchy of controls to an electrical task.`,`Choose appropriately rated tools, instruments and PPE, including measurement categories.`,`Carry out and record a simple risk assessment.`,`Recognise when a task is beyond your competence and must be handed to a qualified person.`],introduction:`Safe practical work is organised before the first tool is picked up. This lesson brings the module together:

- **plan** the task;
- **control** the risks in the right order;
- use tools and instruments **rated** for the job;
- know when to **stop**.

These habits apply in the virtual laboratory as they will in any supervised practical training.`,lecture:`### The hierarchy of controls

Controls are applied in this order, most effective first:

1. **Eliminate:** remove the hazard. Work dead (isolated) instead of live.
2. **Substitute:** use a lower-hazard alternative. Practise on a current-limited, low-voltage bench supply rather than a battery bank or mains.
3. **Engineering controls:** guards, terminal covers, insulated enclosures, fuses, RCDs, interlocks.
4. **Administrative controls:** procedures, permits, training, warning labels, supervision.
5. **PPE:** personal protective equipment, the last line of defence.

### Tools and instruments

- **Insulated tools** rated for 1000 V (to the relevant standard, marked with a double triangle), with no damage to the insulation.
- **Voltage testers and multimeters** rated for the [[measurement-category|measurement category]]:
  - CAT II: appliances and socket outlets;
  - CAT III: distribution boards and fixed installations;
  - CAT IV: the supply origin.
  Higher categories withstand larger transient surges without the meter failing violently. Use fused, shrouded test leads with a matching rating.
- **Inspect before use:** damaged leads, cracked cases or blown fuses take an instrument out of service.

### PPE

The appropriate [[ppe|PPE]] depends on the task's risk assessment:

- safety glasses or a face shield (battery acid, arc flash);
- insulating gloves of the right class for live work (done only where justified and permitted);
- arc-rated clothing where arc-flash energy requires it;
- safety footwear;
- removal of jewellery.

PPE does not make an unsafe method safe.

### Risk assessment

A [[risk-assessment|risk assessment]] answers five questions:

1. What are the **hazards**? (Shock, burns, arc, fire, stored energy, falls, lifting.)
2. Who could be **harmed**, and how?
3. How **likely** is it, and how **severe**?
4. What **controls** will be used (in hierarchy order)?
5. What is the **remaining risk**, and is it acceptable? Record it, and review it if conditions change.

A simple scoring method multiplies likelihood (1–5) by severity (1–5). Scores above an agreed threshold need further controls before work starts.

### Knowing your limits

Stop and ask a qualified, authorised person when:

- the task needs live work;
- the system is unfamiliar or the drawings do not match;
- the isolation cannot be secured;
- you find unexpected voltage;
- the task requires qualifications you do not hold.

**This academy develops knowledge and virtual skill. Real practical work requires supervised training and the qualifications required where you live.**`,technical:`**Measurement categories and transients.** Lightning and switching can put voltage spikes of several kilovolts on supply conductors. The nearer to the supply origin, the larger the spike. Meter categories specify the impulse voltage the meter must withstand. A CAT II meter used on a distribution board (CAT III) could fail and arc into the user's hands.

**Live work.** Most safety regulations permit live work only when it is unreasonable to work dead, when it is reasonable to work live, and when suitable precautions are taken. Fault-finding measurements are the common example. Even then, the work is planned, and done by competent people with the right equipment. In this programme, live measurements happen only in the simulator.

**Risk matrices.** Organisations use 3×3 or 5×5 likelihood-severity matrices with defined action levels. The method in this lesson is a typical example, not a legal standard.`,diagrams:[{id:`risk-hierarchy`,caption:`The hierarchy of controls: elimination is most effective, PPE least.`}],formulas:[{name:`Risk score (typical matrix method)`,expr:`Risk = Likelihood (1–5) × Severity (1–5)`,where:[[`Likelihood`,`1 rare … 5 almost certain`,`—`],[`Severity`,`1 minor … 5 fatal`,`—`]],note:`An illustrative method; organisations define their own matrices and action levels.`}],examples:[{title:`Risk score before and after controls`,problem:`Replacing a battery fuse with the battery connected: likelihood of a short circuit 3, severity 4. After isolating the battery and using insulated tools: likelihood 1, severity 4. Compare the scores.`,steps:[`Before: 3 × 4 = 12 (high: not acceptable)`,`After: 1 × 4 = 4 (low)`],answer:`Isolation (elimination) and insulated tools (engineering) reduce the risk from 12 to 4. Severity stays the same: controls mostly reduce likelihood.`},{title:`Choosing a meter category`,problem:`You will measure at an inverter’s AC output terminals inside a distribution board, and at a socket outlet. Which minimum meter category?`,steps:[`A socket outlet is CAT II`,`A distribution board is CAT III`,`Use the higher requirement for the job`],answer:`A CAT III (or CAT IV) meter rated for the voltage, with matching test leads.`}],applications:`Workplaces require risk assessments for electrical work. Training centres use current-limited practice supplies. The virtual laboratory applies substitution: you learn on simulated circuits with no real hazard before any supervised practical training.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Relying on gloves instead of isolating.`,correction:`PPE is the last line of defence; eliminate the hazard by working dead first.`},{mistake:`Using a cheap CAT II meter in a distribution board.`,correction:`Use an instrument rated for the measurement category and voltage.`},{mistake:`Carrying on when readings do not match expectations.`,correction:`Unexpected readings mean stop, review and ask a qualified person.`}],demo:{title:`Practice supplies in the circuit laboratory`,text:`After passing F1, the circuit laboratory’s practical exercises start with an isolated, current-limited, low-voltage bench supply: substitution in practice.`,link:e.ckt(`Circuit lab: battery and resistor circuit (after F1)`,`ckt-01`)},activities:[e.lab3d(`LF-3K cover and its earth strap`,`lf3k`,`cover`),e.cmp(`Heat sink versus fan`,`heatsink-vs-fan`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which control is the most effective?`,options:[`Eliminating the hazard, for example by working dead`,`PPE`,`Warning signs`,`Training`],answer:0,explain:`Elimination removes the hazard entirely.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A distribution board requires at least which meter category?`,options:[`CAT III`,`CAT I`,`CAT II`,`No category`],answer:0,explain:`Distribution level is CAT III.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Risk score for likelihood 2 and severity 5?`,answer:10,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`2 × 5 = 10.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`You find 120 V on a circuit you isolated. You should…`,options:[`stop, make the area safe and ask a qualified person`,`continue carefully`,`remove the RCD`,`touch it with gloves`],answer:0,explain:`Unexpected voltage means the isolation has failed.`}],summary:[`Hierarchy of controls: eliminate, substitute, engineer, administer, PPE.`,`Use insulated 1000 V tools and meters rated for the measurement category; inspect before use.`,`Risk assessment: hazards, who, likelihood × severity, controls, residual risk, record.`,`Stop and ask when the task is unfamiliar, live, unsecured or unexpected.`,`Virtual practice is not a qualification; real work needs supervised training.`],homework:{id:`F1.M4.L3.hw`,title:`Risk assessment`,brief:`Assess the task “connect a new 24 V battery bank to an off-grid inverter”.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Short-circuit risk score with uncovered terminals and ordinary tools (likelihood 3, severity 4)?`,answer:12,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`3 × 4 = 12.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Score after terminal covers, insulated tools, jewellery removed and the battery fuse open (likelihood 1, severity 4)?`,answer:4,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`1 × 4 = 4.`}],written:[{prompt:`List the controls for this task in hierarchy order.`,model:`Eliminate: connect with the battery fuse/isolator open and the inverter off. Substitute: not applicable (or use pre-made fused cables). Engineering: terminal covers, insulated tools, correct fuse at the battery. Administrative: follow the manufacturer’s sequence (negative last on connection where frame-earthed), supervision. PPE: eye protection, remove jewellery.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The coordination rule for overcurrent protection is…`,options:[`I_b ≤ I_n ≤ I_z`,`I_z ≤ I_n ≤ I_b`,`I_n ≥ I_z`,`I_n = I_b`],answer:0,explain:`Design current ≤ device rating ≤ cable capacity.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fuse for 88 A maximum, 1.25 margin, standard sizes 100/125/150 A (A)?`,answer:125,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 88 = 110 → 125 A.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A battery fuse must have a breaking capacity…`,options:[`above the battery’s prospective short-circuit current at the DC voltage`,`equal to its current rating`,`below 100 A`,`irrelevant for DC`],answer:0,explain:`It must interrupt the largest possible fault.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Earth-fault current, 230 V, Z_s = 1.6 Ω (A)?`,answer:143.75,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`230/1.6 = 143.75 A.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An RCD trips when…`,options:[`line and neutral currents differ by more than its rating`,`current exceeds the cable rating`,`voltage is high`,`the neutral is earthed`],answer:0,explain:`It detects residual current.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Class II equipment…`,options:[`has double insulation and no earth connection`,`must be earthed`,`is for outdoor use only`,`has no insulation`],answer:0,explain:`Double insulation replaces the earth.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`PPE is…`,options:[`the last line of defence after other controls`,`the first control to apply`,`unnecessary if you are careful`,`a substitute for isolation`],answer:0,explain:`Hierarchy of controls.`},{id:`q8`,type:`multi`,prompt:`When should you stop and ask a qualified person? (select all)`,options:[`Unexpected voltage after isolation`,`The isolation cannot be locked`,`The task requires live work`,`The task is going as planned`],answers:[0,1,2],explain:`All except the last.`}]},assignment:{id:`F1.M4.asg`,title:`Module assignment: protecting a battery-inverter circuit`,brief:`A 48 V, 3000 W inverter (η = 0.93) is fed from a 48 V lead-acid bank (cut-off 42 V). Cable options: 70 A, 100 A and 125 A ratings. Fuse sizes: 63, 80, 100, 125 A. Bank fault current: 3 kA.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum DC current at 42 V (A)?`,answer:76.8,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`3000/(0.93 × 42) = 76.8 A.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fuse rating with the 1.25 margin (A)?`,answer:100,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 76.8 = 96 A → 100 A.`},{id:`a3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Smallest cable rating that satisfies I_n ≤ I_z (A)?`,answer:100,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`I_z ≥ 100 A → the 100 A cable (125 A gives margin).`},{id:`a4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The fuse must have a breaking capacity of at least…`,options:[`3 kA at ≥ 57.6 V DC`,`100 A`,`3 kA at 230 V AC only`,`48 A`],answer:0,explain:`Above the prospective fault current at the maximum DC voltage.`}],written:[{prompt:`Where is the fuse installed, and what else protects people in this system?`,model:`At the battery positive terminal, protecting the whole cable run. People are protected by insulation and terminal covers, earthing/bonding of the inverter chassis, an RCD on the AC output where required, and safe isolation before work.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F1.M4.P1`,title:`Protection selection assessment`,description:`Select protective devices and check coordination for a battery-inverter installation. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`f1-protection`,kind:`calc`,title:`Selecting protection`,brief:`A 24 V, 1500 W inverter (η = 0.92, battery cut-off 21 V) has cable rated I_z = 125 A. Battery fault current is 2.4 kA. Fuse sizes: 63, 80, 100, 125, 150 A. The 230 V output circuit has Z_s = 1.4 Ω and a 10 A type B breaker (magnetic trip at 5 × I_n).`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum DC current (A)?`,answer:77.6,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`1500/(0.92 × 21) = 77.6 A.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery fuse rating (A)?`,answer:100,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 77.6 = 97 → 100 A; 100 ≤ 125 ✓.`},{id:`c3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Earth-fault current on the output circuit (A)?`,answer:164.3,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`230/1.4 = 164 A.`},{id:`c4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Does the 10 A type B breaker trip magnetically on that fault?`,options:[`Yes: 164 A > 50 A`,`No`],answer:0,explain:`5 × 10 = 50 A.`},{id:`c5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What protects a person touching line and neutral at the output?`,options:[`Nothing automatic: only insulation and isolation prevent it`,`The RCD`,`The fuse`,`The breaker`],answer:0,explain:`No residual current and far below fuse ratings.`}],explain:`Fuses and breakers are sized from the maximum current with margin and must not exceed the cable rating; earth faults must give enough current to trip quickly; no device protects against line–neutral contact, which is why isolation comes first.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f1-protection`}}]}],exam:{pass:.8,minutes:45,attempts:3,cooldownHours:24,questions:[{id:`e1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A 230 V supply drives current through a body path of 1150 Ω. Current (mA)?`,answer:200,tol:.01,unit:`mA`,explain:`230/1150 = 0.2 A = 200 mA: far above the fibrillation threshold.`},{id:`e2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which quantity most directly determines the harm from an electric shock?`,options:[`The current through the body and its duration`,`The voltage alone`,`The wire colour`,`The power rating of the supply`],answer:0,explain:`Current and duration (and path) determine the effect; voltage drives the current.`},{id:`e3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why are DC arcs harder to extinguish than AC arcs?`,options:[`DC current never passes through zero`,`DC voltage is always higher`,`DC arcs are colder`,`AC has no arcs`],answer:0,explain:`AC passes zero twice per cycle, giving the arc a chance to go out.`},{id:`e4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A 12.8 V battery with 8 mΩ internal resistance is short-circuited. Current (A)?`,answer:1600,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`12.8/0.008 = 1600 A.`},{id:`e5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy stored in 2200 µF at 400 V (J)?`,answer:176,tol:.01,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.0022 × 160 000 = 176 J.`},{id:`e6`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Time for 1000 µF with a 100 kΩ bleeder to fall from 400 V to 50 V (s)?`,answer:207.9,tol:.02,unit:`s`,explain:`τ = 100 s; t = 100 × ln 8 = 207.9 s.`},{id:`e7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A grid-tied PV inverter is switched off at its AC breaker during the day. The PV cables to the inverter are…`,options:[`still live from the array`,`dead`,`dead after 1 s`,`only live at night`],answer:0,explain:`A PV array produces voltage whenever it is lit.`},{id:`e8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What is back-feed?`,options:[`Energy reaching a supposedly dead circuit from another source, such as an inverter or generator`,`A feedback loop in a controller`,`Reverse polarity on a battery`,`A fan blowing backwards`],answer:0,explain:`Back-feed energises circuits from an unexpected direction.`},{id:`e9`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which is the correct start of a safe-isolation procedure?`,options:[`Identify every source supplying the equipment`,`Prove dead`,`Remove the cover`,`Discharge the capacitors`],answer:0,explain:`Sources must be identified before they can be isolated.`},{id:`e10`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A non-contact voltage detector shows no voltage. This means…`,options:[`nothing conclusive: prove dead with a verified two-pole tester`,`the circuit is dead`,`the circuit is DC`,`the RCD has tripped`],answer:0,explain:`Non-contact detectors are screening tools only.`},{id:`e11`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Proving dead on a single-phase AC circuit tests which combinations? (select all)`,options:[`L–N`,`L–E`,`N–E`,`Only the one you are working on`],answers:[0,1,2],explain:`All three combinations.`},{id:`e12`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is the tester re-checked on the proving unit after testing?`,options:[`To prove it did not fail during the test`,`To recharge it`,`It is optional`,`To calibrate it`],answer:0,explain:`A failed tester shows false “dead” readings.`},{id:`e13`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Largest discharge resistor to bring 470 µF from 400 V to 50 V within 10 s (kΩ)?`,answer:10.23,tol:.03,unit:`kΩ`,explain:`R = 10 / (470 × 10⁻⁶ × ln 8) = 10.23 kΩ.`},{id:`e14`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Two technicians work on the same isolated circuit. The isolator carries…`,options:[`two personal locks`,`one shared lock`,`a tag only`,`no lock`],answer:0,explain:`Each worker fits their own lock.`},{id:`e15`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fuse for a 70 A maximum current with the 1.25 margin; sizes 80/100/125 A (A)?`,answer:100,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 70 = 87.5 → 100 A.`},{id:`e16`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A 100 A fuse protects a cable rated 80 A. This is…`,options:[`wrong: I_n must not exceed I_z`,`correct`,`correct for DC only`,`correct if the fuse is slow`],answer:0,explain:`I_b ≤ I_n ≤ I_z.`},{id:`e17`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Earth-fault current with U₀ = 230 V and Z_s = 1.0 Ω (A)?`,answer:230,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`230/1.0 = 230 A.`},{id:`e18`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An RCD protects against…`,options:[`leakage current to earth, including through a person`,`line-to-neutral contact`,`overload of the cable`,`battery short circuits`],answer:0,explain:`It detects the difference between line and neutral currents.`},{id:`e19`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In the hierarchy of controls, which is applied first?`,options:[`Elimination (for example, working dead)`,`PPE`,`Warning signs`,`Training`],answer:0,explain:`Elimination is the most effective control.`},{id:`e20`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which meter category is the minimum for measurements in a distribution board?`,options:[`CAT III`,`CAT I`,`CAT II`,`Any`],answer:0,explain:`Distribution level is CAT III.`}]}};export{t as F1};